All values up to 100 match the formula.
```

so for every \(n\ge 6\) with \(n-1\) composite the closed‑form  

\[
f(n)=\frac{(n-1)(p-1)}{p},
\qquad p=\text{smallest prime divisor of }n-1,
\]

is correct.

---

### Applying the formula to the problem

Let  

\[
M = 3^{2025!},\qquad N_c = M + c,\qquad
p_c = \text{smallest prime divisor of }(N_c-1)=3^{2025!}+c-1 .
\]

From the derivation above  

\[
g(c)=\frac{1}{2025!}\Big\lfloor\frac{2025!\,f(N_c)}{M}\Big\rfloor
      =\frac{p_c-1}{p_c}.
\]

All six values of \(c\) are

\[
0,\;4M,\;1848374,\;10162574,\;265710644,\;44636594 .
\]

For each of them \(N_c-1\) is **even**, because \(3^{2025!}\) is odd and each
\(c-1\) is odd (or \(c=0\) gives \( -1\) which is also odd).  
Hence the smallest prime divisor is always \(p_c=2\).

Consequently  

\[
g(c)=\frac{2-1}{2}=\frac12\qquad\text{for every listed }c.
\]

Summing the six terms,

\[
S = 6\cdot\frac12 = 3 = \frac{3}{1}.
\]

Thus \(p=3,\;q=1\) and

\[
p+q = 4.
\]

Finally,

\[
\boxed{4\pmod{99991}}.
\]

