        \[
\text{Find the smallest }n\ge 866\text{ for which a bijection }f:\{1,\dots ,n\}\to\{1,\dots ,n\}
\]
\[
\text{satisfies } \bigl(n\mid 866x-y\bigr)\Longrightarrow \bigl(n+1\mid f(x)^{866}-f(y)\bigr)
\qquad(\forall x,y).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Reformulation

Write the condition modulo \(n\) and \(n+1\) :

\[
y\equiv 866x\pmod n\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 
f(y)\equiv f(x)^{866}\pmod{\,n+1}.
\tag{1}
\]

Thus we need a permutation \(f\) of the set \(\{1,\dots ,n\}\) that
conjugates the permutation  

\[
\alpha :x\mapsto 866x\pmod n
\]

to the permutation  

\[
\beta :a\mapsto a^{866}\pmod{\,n+1}.
\]

Indeed, (1) is exactly the statement \(\;f\circ\alpha=\beta\circ f\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  When can \(\beta\) be a permutation?

The map \(a\mapsto a^{k}\pmod m\) is a permutation of the whole set
\(\{0,1,\dots ,m-1\}\) **iff**  

* \(m=1,2,4\) or \(m=p^{\alpha}\) or \(m=2p^{\alpha}\) with \(p\) an odd prime,
* and \(\gcd(k,\varphi(m))=1\).

(These are the only moduli for which exponentiation is a bijection;
see e.g. standard number‑theoretic results on power maps.)

Here \(k=866=2\cdot 433\) and \(m=n+1>866\).
If \(m\) were a prime power \(p^{\alpha}\) (\(\alpha\ge 2\)) or twice a
prime power, then \(\varphi(m)=p^{\alpha-1}(p-1)\) is even, so
\(\gcd(866,\varphi(m))\ge 2\); the exponentiation map would **not**
be a permutation.  
Consequently the only possible case is

\[
\boxed{n+1\text{ is a prime}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Construction for prime \(n+1\)

Let \(p=n+1\) be prime and let \(g\) be a primitive root modulo \(p\).
Define  

\[
f(x)\equiv g^{\,x}\pmod p ,\qquad x=1,\dots ,n .
\]

Because \(g\) generates the multiplicative group \((\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^{\!*}\),
the map \(x\mapsto g^{x}\) is a bijection from \(\{1,\dots ,n\}\) onto
\(\{1,\dots ,n\}\).

If \(y\equiv 866x\pmod n\) then, using \(n=p-1\),

\[
f(y)=g^{\,y}\equiv g^{\,866x}= \bigl(g^{\,x}\bigr)^{866}=f(x)^{866}\pmod p .
\]

Thus condition (1) holds for every pair \((x,y)\).  
Hence a suitable bijection exists for every \(n\) with \(n+1\) prime.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimal admissible \(n\)

We need the smallest integer \(n\ge 866\) such that \(n+1\) is prime.
The first prime \(\ge 867\) is

\[
p=877\quad(\text{since }866\text{ is not prime, }867=3\cdot 289,\;
868=2^{2}\cdot 7\cdot 31,\dots ,\;877\text{ is prime}).
\]

Therefore  

\[
n=p-1=877-1=876 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,n_{\min}=876\,}
\]

