        For the three dice let the first roll be \((a,b,c)\) (each uniformly \(1\!-\!6\)).
Kevin may re‑roll exactly two of the dice, i.e. he keeps one die and replaces the other
two with fresh independent dice.

---

### 1.  Probability of obtaining sum \(7\) after the re‑roll

If the kept die shows the value \(k\) then the two new dice must sum to  

\[
7-k .
\]

For ordered pairs \((x,y)\) with \(x,y\in\{1,\dots ,6\}\) the number of pairs that give a
sum \(s\) is  

\[
\#\{(x,y):x+y=s\}= 
\begin{cases}
s-1,&2\le s\le 7,\\[2pt]
13-s,&8\le s\le 12,\\[2pt]
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

Thus the probability, given the kept value \(k\), is  

\[
f(k)=\frac{\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-k\}}{6^2}
=
\begin{array}{c|c}
k & f(k) \\ \hline
1 & \dfrac{5}{36}\\
2 & \dfrac{4}{36}= \dfrac{1}{9}\\
3 & \dfrac{3}{36}= \dfrac{1}{12}\\
4 & \dfrac{2}{36}= \dfrac{1}{18}\\
5 & \dfrac{1}{36}\\
6 & 0
\end{array}
\]

Because \(f(k)\) decreases as \(k\) increases, the optimal choice is to **keep the
smallest die** among the three.

---

### 2.  Distribution of the minimum of three dice  

Let \(X=\min\{a,b,c\}\).  
\[
\Pr(X\ge k)=\Bigl(\frac{7-k}{6}\Bigr)^{\!3},\qquad k=1,\dots ,6,
\]
so  

\[
\Pr(X=k)=\Pr(X\ge k)-\Pr(X\ge k+1)
      =\Bigl(\frac{7-k}{6}\Bigr)^{\!3}-\Bigl(\frac{6-k}{6}\Bigr)^{\!3}.
\]

Explicitly  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
k & \Pr(X=k) \\ \hline
1 & \dfrac{91}{216}\\
2 & \dfrac{61}{216}\\
3 & \dfrac{37}{216}\\
4 & \dfrac{19}{216}\\
5 & \dfrac{7}{216}\\
6 & \dfrac{1}{216}
\end{array}
\]

---

### 3.  Expected success probability for \(n=2\)

\[
p=\mathbb{E}[\,f(X)\,]
   =\sum_{k=1}^{6} f(k)\,\Pr(X=k)
   =\frac{5}{36}\frac{91}{216}
    +\frac{4}{36}\frac{61}{216}
    +\frac{3}{36}\frac{37}{216}
    +\frac{2}{36}\frac{19}{216}
    +\frac{1}{36}\frac{7}{216}.
\]

Evaluating,

\[
p=\frac{855}{7776}= \frac{95}{864}.
\]

---

### 4.  Compute \(216p\)

\[
216p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \frac{95}{4}}.
\]

(If a decimal answer is preferred, it is \(23.75\).)

