        The three dice are rolled first.  
Let the values be \((a,b,c)\).  
Kevin must re‑roll exactly two dice, keeping one die unchanged.

If the kept die shows \(v\), the two re‑rolled dice must sum to \(7-v\).
For a fixed \(v\) the number of ordered pairs \((x,y)\) with \(x+y=7-v\) is  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
v & \#\{(x,y):x+y=7-v\}\\ \hline
1 & 5\\
2 & 4\\
3 & 3\\
4 & 2\\
5 & 1\\
6 & 0
\end{array}
\]

Denote this count by \(c(v)\).  
Given \((a,b,c)\) Kevin will keep the die that maximises \(c(v)\); the
probability of finally obtaining a total of \(7\) is therefore  

\[
\frac{\max\{c(a),c(b),c(c)\}}{36}.
\]

Hence, with the initial three dice uniformly distributed,

\[
p=\frac1{216}\sum_{a,b,c=1}^{6}\frac{\max\{c(a),c(b),c(c)\}}{36}
      =\frac{1}{216\cdot36}\sum_{a,b,c}\max\{c(a),c(b),c(c)\}.
\]

Let  

\[
S=\sum_{a,b,c}\max\{c(a),c(b),c(c)\}.
\]

Counting triples according to the largest value of \(c(v)\):

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{max }c & \text{condition on the dice} & \text{number of triples}\\ \hline
5 & \text{at least one }1 & 216-5^{3}=91\\
4 & \text{no }1,\text{ at least one }2 & 5^{3}-4^{3}=61\\
3 & \text{no }1,2,\text{ at least one }3 & 4^{3}-3^{3}=37\\
2 & \text{no }1,2,3,\text{ at least one }4 & 3^{3}-2^{3}=19\\
1 & \text{no }1,2,3,4,\text{ at least one }5 & 2^{3}-1^{3}=7\\
0 & \text{all three dice are }6 & 1
\end{array}
\]

Thus  

\[
S=5\cdot91+4\cdot61+3\cdot37+2\cdot19+1\cdot7+0\cdot1=855.
\]

Consequently  

\[
p=\frac{S}{216\cdot36}= \frac{855}{7776}= \frac{95}{864},
\qquad
216p=\frac{216\cdot95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

