        The optimal strategy when exactly two dice may be re‑thrown is to keep the die showing the smallest value (the minimum of the three dice).  

For a kept value \(x\) the required sum on the two re‑rolled dice is \(7-x\).  
The probability that two fair dice sum to \(s\) is  

\[
\frac{1}{36}\times\begin{cases}
1,2,3,4,5,6,5,4,3,2,1 & (s=2,3,\dots ,12)\\[2pt]
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

Hence  

\[
p(x)=\frac{\#\text{pairs with sum }7-x}{36}
      =\frac{5,4,3,2,1,0}{36}\quad\text{for }x=1,2,3,4,5,6 .
\]

Thus for each initial triple we keep the minimum die value \(m\) and obtain probability \(p(m)\).  
Let \(N_m\) be the number of triples whose minimum equals \(m\):

\[
N_m=(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\quad(m=1,\dots ,6)
\]
\[
N_1=91,\;N_2=61,\;N_3=37,\;N_4=19,\;N_5=7,\;N_6=1 .
\]

The total of the probabilities over all \(216\) equally likely triples is  

\[
\sum_{m=1}^{6} N_m\,p(m)
   =\frac{91\cdot5+61\cdot4+37\cdot3+19\cdot2+7\cdot1}{36}
   =\frac{855}{36}= \frac{95}{4}.
\]

Since there are \(216\) initial outcomes, the overall probability is  

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\cdot\frac{95}{4}= \frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally  

\[
216\,p = \frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

