* In every *middle* row \(r\;(2\le r\le 3032)\) there is **exactly one** invisible teleportation gate.  
* No two gates are in the same column, i.e. the set of columns that contain a gate is a set of
  \(3031\) distinct numbers taken from \(\{1,\dots ,3032\}\).

Thus there are  

\[
\underbrace{3031}_{\text{middle rows}} \text{ gates } \qquad
\text{and}\qquad 
\underbrace{1}_{\text{column without a gate}} \text{ safe column.}
\]

When the explorer steps on a gate he is instantly sent back to the square in the first row from which he started that attempt; the gate’s position is then known forever.  
He may move up, down, left or right and may walk over already visited squares as many times as he wishes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Lower bound – why we need at least \(3032\) attempts  

*During a single attempt the explorer can learn the position of **at most one** gate* – the moment he touches a gate he is teleported back and the attempt ends.  
Hence, to learn the positions of all \(3031\) gates he needs at least \(3031\) attempts.

Even after the last gate has been discovered the explorer is still standing in the first row.
He must now walk to the bottom row without ever touching a gate, and that requires **one more** attempt (the moment he touches a gate he would be teleported again).  

Consequently any strategy must use at least  

\[
3031\;(\text{discover all gates})+1\;(\text{final safe walk})=3032
\]

attempts in the worst case.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  A strategy that always succeeds in exactly \(3032\) attempts  

The explorer keeps a list **Known** of columns that already contain a discovered gate.  
Initially **Known** is empty.

For attempt \(i=1,2,\dots\)

1. **Choose a fresh column**  
   Pick the smallest column number \(c\) that is **not** in **Known**.  
   (Because the gates occupy distinct columns, each column can contain at most one gate, so a column that has already been discovered will never hide another gate.)

2. **Go straight down that column**  
   From the starting square in row 1 move only downwards (no horizontal moves).  
   *If a gate lies in column \(c\)*, the explorer will step on it at the unique row where it is placed, be teleported back to the start, and **add \(c\) to Known**.  
   *If no gate lies in column \(c\)*, the explorer reaches row 3033 and exits the maze – the attempt is successful.

3. **Repeat** the next attempt with the updated set **Known**.

Because the columns of the gates are all different, each time the explorer chooses a column not yet in **Known** he is guaranteed to encounter a **new** gate (unless that column is the unique safe column).  

Hence, in the worst‑case placement the sequence of events is

* attempts 1 … 3031 : each chosen column contains a gate → a new gate is discovered and the attempt ends,
* attempt 3032 : the only column left is the safe one → the explorer reaches the bottom row and finishes.

Thus the described procedure always succeeds, and it never needs more than \(3032\) attempts.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Generalisation  

If a maze has  

* \(R\) rows (\(R\ge 3\)),  
* \(C\) columns (\(C=R-1\)),  

and there is exactly one gate in each of the \(R-2\) middle rows, all in distinct columns, then the same reasoning gives  

\[
k_{\min}= (R-2) + 1 = R-1 .
\]

For the concrete numbers of the problem (\(R=3033\)) we obtain \(k_{\min}=3032\).

