### 2.  A strategy that always works in 3032 attempts  

Number the columns \(1,2,\dots ,3032\).

```
for c = 1,2,…,3032
        start in column c of the first row
        go straight down (never move left/right)
        if a gate is met → the attempt ends, the gate’s column is known
        if the bottom row 3033 is reached → the maze is passed
```

*If the gate in column \(c\) lies in some middle row, the explorer will
hit it, be teleported back, and will have learned that column \(c\) is
occupied.*  
*If column \(c\) is the unique column without a gate, the explorer never
steps on a gate and reaches row 3033, finishing the maze.*

Because there are exactly \(3031\) occupied columns, the empty column is
among the \(3032\) columns that are tried.  
Consequently the explorer certainly succeeds **no later than the
\(3032^{\text{nd}}\) attempt**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Why no strategy can guarantee success earlier  

Consider any deterministic strategy \(S\).  
During an attempt the explorer can only discover **one** new gate,
because the moment a gate is touched the attempt ends.

Assume the strategy would guarantee success in at most \(k<3032\)
attempts for **every** possible placement of the gates.
We shall construct a placement that forces the explorer to fail on each of
the first \(k\) attempts, contradicting the assumption.

* For the first attempt the strategy chooses some column \(c_{1}\) to
enter the second row.  
  Put a gate in column \(c_{1}\) (this is possible because no column has
  been used yet).

* After the first failure the explorer knows that column \(c_{1}\) is
occupied.  
  In the second attempt the strategy must choose a column different from
  \(c_{1}\); call it \(c_{2}\).  
  Place a gate in column \(c_{2}\) (still unused).

* Continue this process. After the \(i\)-th failure the columns
\(c_{1},c_{2},\dots ,c_{i}\) are already occupied, and the strategy
must pick a new column \(c_{i+1}\neq c_{j}\;(j\le i)\).  
  Put a gate in column \(c_{i+1}\).

Because the columns are required to be distinct, this construction can be
carried out for as many steps as there are columns, i.e. for
\(3031\) steps.  Thus for any \(k\le 3031\) we can force the explorer to
fail on each of the first \(k\) attempts.  The only way to be certain of
success is to survive the attempt that uses the **unique empty column**,
which can be forced to be the last column examined.

Hence any strategy needs at least \(3031\) possible failures plus the final
successful attempt, i.e. **\(3032\) attempts in the worst case**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

