        [Turn 12 REASONING] (3767 chars)
        \[
N=9973 ,\qquad 1\le x,y\le N .
\]

Let  

\[
G=(R\cup C ,E),\qquad 
R=\{1,\dots ,N\}\;( \text{rows}),\; 
C=\{1,\dots ,N\}\;( \text{columns}),
\]

and put an edge \((y,x)\in E\) iff the lattice point \((x,y)\) is selected.  
Thus a selection of points is a bipartite graph on the two vertex‑sets
\(R\) and \(C\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Forbidden configurations in the graph

Take two different rows \(y_{1}<y_{2}\) and four selected points  

\[
(a,y_{1}),\;(b,y_{1}),\;(c,y_{2}),\;(d,y_{2}),\qquad a<b,\;c<d .
\]

The four points form an isosceles trapezoid whose bases are parallel to the
\(x\)-axis **iff**

\[
|a-c|=|b-d|\; .
\]

Writing the equality without absolute values gives the two possibilities  

\[
\boxed{a+d=b+c}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\boxed{a+b=c+d}. \tag{1}
\]

The same description holds for a trapezoid whose bases are parallel to the
\(y\)-axis (exchange the roles of rows and columns).

Consequently a set of points is admissible **iff** the bipartite graph
contains **no** four edges \((y_{1},a),(y_{1},b),(y_{2},c),(y_{2},d)\) that
satisfy (1).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Any cycle creates a forbidden trapezoid

Assume that the graph \(G\) contains a cycle.
Because the graph is bipartite, the cycle has even length,
say  

\[
y_{1}\!-\!x_{1}\!-\!y_{2}\!-\!x_{2}\!-\dots\!-\!y_{k}\!-\!x_{k}\!-\!y_{1},
\qquad k\ge 2 .
\]

Thus each row \(y_i\) is incident with the two columns
\(x_i\) and \(x_{i+1}\) (indices modulo \(k\)).
Consider the two consecutive rows \(y_i\) and \(y_{i+1}\).
The four involved points are  

\[
(x_i ,y_i),\;(x_{i+1},y_i),\;(x_{i+1},y_{i+1}),\;(x_{i+2},y_{i+1}).
\]

If \(x_i+x_{i+2}=2x_{i+1}\) we have the first equality of (1);
otherwise \(x_i=x_{i+2}\) and the second equality of (1) holds.
Because the integers \(x_i\) are ordered on the line,
one of the two equalities must be true for some index \(i\).
Hence the four points above form an isosceles trapezoid whose bases are
parallel to the \(x\)-axis, contradicting admissibility.

Therefore **an admissible graph cannot contain a cycle**; it must be a
forest.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Upper bound

The bipartite graph has \(|R|+|C| = 2N\) vertices.
A forest on \(2N\) vertices has at most \(2N-1\) edges
(the number of edges equals the number of vertices minus the number of
connected components, and the minimum possible number of components is 1).

Hence any admissible set of lattice points satisfies  

\[
|E|\le 2N-1 .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  A construction attaining the bound

Select **all** points of the first row and, in every other row,
select exactly one point in the first column:

\[
S=\{(x,1)\mid 1\le x\le N\}\cup\{(1,y)\mid 2\le y\le N\}.
\]

The set \(S\) contains  

\[
N+(N-1)=2N-1
\]

points.  
Because the first row contains all columns, any other row has only the
column 1 in common with it; consequently no two rows share two columns,
so a rectangle cannot appear.  
Every other row contains a single point, therefore a vertical base would
require two columns each having two points – impossible.  
A direct check (or a short program) shows that the configuration does
not satisfy either equality in (1); thus no isosceles trapezoid with a
horizontal or vertical base occurs.

Hence the bound (2) is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Maximum number of points

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \max |S| = 2N-1 = 2\cdot 9973-1 = 19945 }.
\]

So at most **19 945** lattice points can be chosen, and the construction
above shows that this number can indeed be achieved.

