Let a flip be a visit to a coin.  
If a coin is visited an odd number of times its final side is opposite to the
initial side, and if it is visited an even number of times it stays the same.
Hence

* every odd‑numbered coin (initially tails) must be visited **odd** times,
* every even‑numbered coin (initially heads) must be visited **even** times.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  From flips to a walk on a line

Number the coins \(1,2,\dots ,N\).  
A move consists of moving to a neighbour and flipping that neighbour, so a
sequence of moves is a walk on the path graph  

\[
1\!-\!2\!-\!3\!-\!\dots\!-\!N .
\]

If the walk uses the edge \((i,i+1)\) \(x_i\) times, the degree of vertex
\(i\) in the multigraph formed by the walk is  

\[
d_i = x_{i-1}+x_i\qquad (x_0=x_N=0).
\]

The number of flips of vertex \(i\) equals  

\[
\text{flips}(i)=
\begin{cases}
1+x_{i-1}+x_i & (i\text{ is the first vertex)}\\[2mm]
x_{i-1}+x_i   & (i\text{ otherwise}).
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Parity conditions on the degrees  

From the required parity of flips we obtain the following congruences
(mod 4) for the degrees.

