        **Answer: \(\displaystyle 39404\)**

**Explanation**

Let  

\[
a_1=1\le a_2\le\cdots\le a_{2025}=199 ,\qquad 
d_i=a_{i+1}-a_i\;(i=1,\dots ,2024)
\]

so each \(d_i\ge 0\) and  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{2024} d_i = a_{2025}-a_1 = 198 .
\]

The required expression is  

\[
f =\sum_{i=1}^{2025} a_i^{2}
      -\sum_{i=1}^{2023} a_i a_{i+2}.
\]

Using \(a_{i+2}=a_i+d_i+d_{i+1}\),

\[
a_{i+2}-a_i = d_i+d_{i+1},
\qquad
(a_i-a_{i+2})^{2}=(d_i+d_{i+1})^{2}.
\]

A short algebraic manipulation gives  

\[
\boxed{\,f=
\frac12\Bigl[\sum_{i=1}^{2023}(d_i+d_{i+1})^{2}
          +a_{2024}^{2}+a_{2025}^{2}+a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}\Bigr] } .
\]

Expanding the squares and substituting \(a_{1}=1,\;a_{2}=1+d_1,\;
a_{2025}=199,\;a_{2024}=199-d_{2024}\) yields the equivalent form  

\[
f = 199^{2}+1
    + d_1-199\,d_{2024}
    +\sum_{i=1}^{2024} d_i^{2}
    +\sum_{i=1}^{2023} d_i d_{i+1}.
\tag{1}
\]

All terms in the last two sums are non‑negative, while the linear term
\(-199\,d_{2024}\) is strongly negative.
Hence to minimise (1) we should make \(d_{2024}\) as large as possible
and all other \(d_i\) as small as possible.

Because \(\sum d_i =198\), the extreme choice  

\[
d_{2024}=198,\qquad d_i=0\;(i\neq2024)
\]

satisfies the constraints.  
For this choice

\[
\sum d_i^{2}=198^{2},\qquad 
\sum d_i d_{i+1}=0,\qquad 
d_1=0,
\]

and (1) gives  

\[
f = 199^{2}+1 +0-199\cdot198 + 198^{2}
   = 39601+1-39402+39204
   = 39404 .
\]

The corresponding sequence is  

\[
a_1=a_2=\dots =a_{2024}=1,\qquad a_{2025}=199,
\]

which indeed respects the monotonicity and endpoint conditions.

Any redistribution of the total increase \(198\) away from the last
step would increase the non‑negative quadratic terms while reducing the
large negative term \(-199 d_{2024}\), thereby raising the value of \(f\).
Thus the above sequence yields the global minimum.

\[
\boxed{f_{\min}=39404}
\]

