        The expression can be written as a rank‑one quadratic form  

\[
F(\mathbf x)=\sum_{i,j=1}^{2025}\frac{2i-1}{2j-1}\,x_i x_j
          =\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i\Bigr)
           \Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{2025}\frac{x_j}{2j-1}\Bigr)
          =u\;v ,
\]

where  

\[
u=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}(2i-1)x_i ,\qquad   
v=\sum_{i=1}^{2025}\frac{x_i}{2i-1}.
\]

Because \(x_i\ge 0\) and \(\sum x_i=1\), the pair \((u,v)\) lies in the convex hull of the points  

\[
\bigl(2i-1,\; \frac1{2i-1}\bigr),\qquad i=1,\dots ,2025 .
\]

For a rank‑one form the KKT conditions give  

\[
(2i-1)v+\frac{u}{2i-1}= \lambda\qquad\text{whenever }x_i>0 .
\]

If more than two components of \(\mathbf x\) were positive, the above equality could not hold for distinct \(i\).  
Hence at an optimum at most two \(x_i\)’s are non‑zero.

---

### Two‑point mixture  

Let the positive components be at indices \(i<j\) with weights \(p\) and \(1-p\) (\(0\le p\le1\)).  
Set  

\[
a=2i-1,\qquad A=2j-1,\qquad d=A-a>0 .
\]

Then  

\[
u=p\,a+(1-p)A,\qquad 
v=p\frac1a+(1-p)\frac1A,
\]

and

\[
F(p)=uv=1+\frac{d^{2}}{aA}\,p(1-p).
\]

Since \(p(1-p)\le\frac14\) with equality at \(p=\frac12\),

\[
\max_{p}F(p)=1+\frac{(A-a)^{2}}{4aA}
           =1+\frac{(j-i)^{2}}{(2i-1)(2j-1)} .
\]

Thus for each pair \((i,j)\),

\[
S_{i,j}=1+\frac{(j-i)^{2}}{(2i-1)(2j-1)} .
\]

---

### Choosing the best pair  

For a fixed difference \(\Delta=j-i\), the denominator \((2i-1)(2j-1)\) is smallest when \(i\) is smallest, i.e. \(i=1\).  
Hence the maximal value is attained for the extreme pair \((i,j)=(1,2025)\) (the largest possible \(\Delta=2024\)).

\[
S_{\max}=1+\frac{2024^{2}}{(2\cdot1-1)(2\cdot2025-1)}
        =1+\frac{2024^{2}}{1\cdot4049}
        =\frac{4049+2024^{2}}{4049}
        =\frac{4100625}{4049}.
\]

The optimal distribution is  

\[
x_{1}=x_{2025}=\frac12,\qquad x_{k}=0\;(k\neq1,2025).
\]

Since \(4049\) is prime and does not divide \(4100625\), the fraction is already in lowest terms.  

Therefore  

\[
m=4100625,\qquad n=4049,\qquad m+n=4100625+4049=4104674.
\]

