\]

For a probability vector \(x\) (\(x_i\ge 0,\;\sum x_i =1\))

\[
A =\sum a_i x_i =\mathbb{E}[a],\qquad 
B =\sum b_i x_i =\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac1a\right].
\]

Hence we must maximise  

\[
F(x)=\mathbb{E}[a]\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac1a\right].
\]

---

### 1.  At most two non‑zero coordinates

Using Lagrange multipliers for the constraint \(\sum x_i=1\),

\[
a_k B + b_k A =\lambda\qquad\text{for every }k\text{ with }x_k>0 .
\]

Since the left‑hand side is linear in \((a_k,b_k)\), at most two different
indices can satisfy the same \(\lambda\).  
Thus an extremal point of the feasible region uses at most two
coordinates.  

Consequently we may restrict to a distribution that puts mass \(p\) on
\(a\) and \(1-p\) on \(b\) (\(a<b\)).  

\[
F(p)=\bigl(p a+(1-p)b\bigr)\bigl(p/a+(1-p)/b\bigr).
\]

---

### 2.  Optimal weight for two points  

