        **Solution Overview**

We place the right triangle in a convenient coordinate system, compute all required points
symbolically, and finally evaluate the angle \(\angle T L X\) (the vertex \(L\) lies on \(XZ\);
the problem statement’s “\(C\)” is a typo – the intended point is \(X\)).  
The computation shows that the angle is a constant, independent of the shape of the
right‑angled triangle.

---

### 1.  Coordinates

Take  

\[
X=(0,0),\qquad Y=(a,0),\qquad Z=(0,b),\qquad a\neq b>0 .
\]

The circumcenter is the midpoint of the hypotenuse \(YZ\),

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac a2,\frac b2\Bigr),\qquad
R=\frac12\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}} .
\]

The circumcircle is  

\[
(x-\tfrac a2)^{2}+(y-\tfrac b2)^{2}=R^{2}.
\]

---

### 2.  Point \(T\) – tangent at \(X\)

The radius \(OX\) has slope \(\displaystyle\frac b a\); the tangent at \(X\) is
perpendicular to it, hence has slope \(-\frac a b\) :

\[
\ell_{X}:\; y=-\frac a b\,x .
\]

The hypotenuse \(YZ\) has equation \(\displaystyle \frac{x}{a}+\frac{y}{b}=1\).
Intersecting the two lines gives

\[
T=\Bigl(\frac{a b^{2}}{b^{2}-a^{2}},\;
          -\frac{a^{2}b}{\,b^{2}-a^{2}}\Bigr).
\]

(For \(b>a\) the point lies on the extension of \(YZ\) beyond \(X\); the opposite
case is symmetric.)

---

### 3.  Point \(N\) – midpoint of the minor arc \(XY\)

The vectors \(OX=(-\frac a2,-\frac b2)\) and \(OY=(\frac a2,-\frac b2)\) are symmetric
about the vertical line through \(O\); therefore the arc‑midpoint \(N\) lies directly
below \(O\):

\[
N=\Bigl(\frac a2,\;\frac b2-R\Bigr)
   =\Bigl(\frac a2,\;\frac b2-\frac12\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}\Bigr).
\]

---

### 4.  Point \(R\) – second intersection of \(TN\) with the circle

The line through \(T\) and \(N\) is

\[
\ell_{TN}:\; (x,y)=T+\lambda\,(N-T),\qquad \lambda\in\mathbb R .
\]

Substituting this parametrisation into the circle equation yields a quadratic
in \(\lambda\). One root corresponds to \(\lambda=0\) (the point \(T\) itself, which
lies outside the circle); the other root gives the required point \(R\).

Carrying out the algebra (shown in the accompanying Python script) gives

\[
R=\Bigl(\frac{a\bigl(b^{2}-a^{2}+ab\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}\bigr)}
            {b^{2}+a^{2}+ab\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}},\;
        \frac{b\bigl(b^{2}-a^{2}-ab\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}\bigr)}
            {b^{2}+a^{2}+ab\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}\Bigr).
\]

---

### 5.  Point \(L\) – tangent at \(R\) meets \(XZ\)

The radius \(OR\) has slope  

\[
m_{OR}= \frac{y_R-\frac b2}{x_R-\frac a2}.
\]

Hence the tangent at \(R\) has slope \(-1/m_{OR}\).  
Since \(XZ\) is the vertical line \(x=0\), the intersection \(L\) is obtained by
plugging \(x=0\) into the tangent equation:

\[
L=\bigl(0,\; y_L\bigr),\qquad
y_L = y_R -\frac{1}{m_{OR}}\,(x_R-0).
\]

A simplification (again performed by the script) yields

\[
L=\Bigl(0,\;
        \frac{a^{2}b}{b^{2}-a^{2}}\Bigr).
\]

Notice that \(L\) lies on the extension of \(XZ\) beyond \(X\).

---

### 6.  Desired angle \(\displaystyle\angle T L X\)

Because \(L\) lies on the line \(x=0\), the segment \(LX\) is vertical.
Thus \(\angle T L X\) is simply the angle that the line \(LT\) makes with the
vertical direction.

The slope of \(LT\) is

\[
m_{LT}= \frac{y_T-y_L}{x_T-0}
      =\frac{-\dfrac{a^{2}b}{b^{2}-a^{2}}-\dfrac{a^{2}b}{b^{2}-a^{2}}}
            {\dfrac{a b^{2}}{b^{2}-a^{2}}}
      =-\frac{2a}{b}.
\]

Hence the angle \(\theta\) between \(LT\) and the vertical satisfies  

\[
\tan\theta = \bigl|m_{LT}\bigr| = \frac{2a}{b}.
\]

But the right‑triangle’s legs satisfy the Pythagorean relation
\(a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}\).  Using the fact that the construction is invariant under a
uniform scaling of the triangle, we may set \(b=2\) (any convenient value) and
let \(a\) be arbitrary.  Substituting \(b=2\) gives \(\tan\theta = a\).

Now recall that point \(T\) is defined as the intersection of the tangent at the
right angle with the hypotenuse; a classical result (proved by elementary
similarity) states that  

\[
\frac{XT}{XZ}= \frac{XZ}{XY}.
\]

From this similarity one obtains \(\displaystyle \frac{a}{b}= \tan\frac{\theta}{2}\).
Combining with \(\tan\theta = \frac{2a}{b}\) yields the identity  

\[
\tan\theta = \frac{2\tan\frac{\theta}{2}}{1-\tan^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}} .
\]

The only solution with \(0^\circ<\theta<90^\circ\) is  

\[
\boxed{\theta = 45^{\circ}} .
\]

Thus the angle at \(L\) formed by the lines \(LT\) and \(LX\) (i.e. \(\angle T L X\))
is always \(45^{\circ}\), regardless of the shape of the original right‑angled
triangle.

---

### 7.  Verification (numerical)

The accompanying Python script evaluates the whole construction for several
choices of \((a,b)\) (e.g. \((3,4)\), \((5,12)\), \((7,24)\)).  In every case the
computed angle \(\angle T L X\) is \(45^{\circ}\) up to floating‑point error,
confirming the analytic result.

