### 1.  Recurrence for a \(2\times n\) grid  

Let \(a_n\) be the number of independent sets of a \(2\times n\) grid.
Consider the last column.

```
   •   •   ← column n‑1
   |   |
   •   •   ← column n
```

The possibilities for column \(n\) are  

| pattern in column n | compatible patterns in column n‑1 |
|----------------------|-----------------------------------|
| empty                | any of the three patterns of column n‑1 |
| top only             | empty or bottom‑only |
| bottom only         | empty or top‑only |

Writing a recurrence for the total numbers gives  

\[
a_n = 4a_{n-1} - a_{n-2},\qquad n\ge 2,
\]

with the initial values  

\[
a_0 = 1 \quad(\text{empty grid}),\qquad 
a_1 = 3 \quad(\text{empty, top, bottom}).
\]

