        The condition “no two chosen numbers differ by 1 or 21’’ can be described by a graph.

* Vertices : the numbers \(1,2,\dots ,42\).
* Edges   : \(\{i,i+1\}\;(1\le i\le 41)\)   (difference 1) and  
            \(\{i,i+21\}\;(1\le i\le 21)\) (difference 21).

If we write the numbers in two rows  

\[
\begin{array}{cccccc}
1&2&\dots &21\\[2pt]
22&23&\dots &42
\end{array}
\]

the edges are exactly the horizontal edges of each row and the
vertical edges joining the two rows in the same column.
Hence the graph is the **ladder graph** \(L_{21}=P_{21}\square K_{2}\)
( two rows of 21 vertices, each column forming a “rung’’).

Choosing a subset with the required property is the same as choosing an
**independent set** of this ladder graph.
Thus we have to count the independent sets of \(L_{21}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Dynamic programming on a column

For each column we may have three possible states  

* \(0\) – no vertex of the column is chosen,
* \(T\) – the top vertex is chosen,
* \(B\) – the bottom vertex is chosen.

(The state “both’’ is forbidden because the two vertices of a column are
adjacent – they differ by 21.)

If a column is in state  

* \(0\) the next column may be in any of the three states,
* \(T\) the next column may be \(0\) or \(B\) (the top of the next column
  cannot be chosen),
* \(B\) the next column may be \(0\) or \(T\).

Hence the transition matrix between successive columns is  

\[
M=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&1\\[2pt]
1&0&1\\[2pt]
1&1&0
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\text{rows}= \{0,T,B\},\; \text{columns}= \{0,T,B\}.
\]

Let  

\[
x_n,\;y_n,\;z_n
\]

be the numbers of admissible sequences of length \(n\) ending in the
states \(0,T,B\) respectively.  
From the matrix we obtain the recurrences  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{n+1}&=x_n+y_n+z_n,\\
y_{n+1}&=x_n+z_n,\\
z_{n+1}&=x_n+y_n .
\end{aligned}
\]

For the first column \(x_1=y_1=z_1=1\) (any of the three states is
possible).  
The total number of independent sets for a ladder of length \(n\) is  

\[
a_n = x_n+y_n+z_n .
\]

From the three equations we obtain a single recurrence for \(a_n\):

\[
a_{n}=2a_{n-1}+a_{n-2},\qquad a_0=1,\;a_1=3 .
\]

