        The condition “no two chosen numbers differ by \(1\) or \(21\)” can be viewed as a graph‑theoretic problem.

---

### 1.  The graph

Place the numbers \(1,\dots ,42\) in two rows  

\[
\begin{array}{cccccc}
1 & 2 & \dots & 20 & 21\\[2pt]
22&23&\dots &41 &42
\end{array}
\]

* Horizontal edges join consecutive numbers in each row  
  (difference \(1\)).
* A vertical edge joins the two numbers in the same column  
  (difference \(21\)).

Thus we obtain the **ladder graph** \(L_{21}\) – a \(2\times 21\) grid.
A subset of \(\{1,\dots ,42\}\) satisfying the condition is exactly an
*independent set* of this graph (no two vertices of the set are adjacent).

Hence we must count the independent sets of the ladder graph with
\(n=21\) rungs.

---

### 2.  Dynamic programming on columns  

For each column \(i\) ( \(1\le i\le 21\) ) there are three possible
states  

* \(0\): none of the two vertices of the column is chosen,
* \(1\): the upper vertex (the number \(i\)) is chosen,
* \(2\): the lower vertex (the number \(i+21\)) is chosen.

The vertical edge forbids states \(1\) and \(2\) together, so a column
can be in exactly one of the three states above.

Horizontal edges give the transition restrictions

* from state \(0\) we may go to \(0,1,2\);
* from state \(1\) we may go to \(0,2\) (cannot stay in the upper row);
* from state \(2\) we may go to \(0,1\) (cannot stay in the lower row).

Therefore the transition matrix is  

\[
M=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&1\\
1&0&1\\
1&1&0
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\text{rows = previous state, columns = next state}.
\]

If \(v=[1,1,1]\) is the vector of possibilities for the first column,
the number of admissible state sequences for \(n\) columns is  

\[
\mathbf{c}_n = v\,M^{\,n-1},
\]

and the total number of independent sets (all subsets) is  

\[
a_n = \mathbf{c}_n\cdot\mathbf{1}
      = v\,M^{\,n-1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\1\end{pmatrix}.
\]

---

### 3.  Closed form

The eigenvalues of \(M\) are \(-1,\;1-\sqrt2,\;1+\sqrt2\).  
Carrying out the matrix power (or solving the linear recurrence) yields

\[
\boxed{a_n=\frac{(1+\sqrt2)^{\,n+1}+(1-\sqrt2)^{\,n+1}}{2}} .
\]

---

### 4.  Evaluation for \(n=21\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{21}
&=\frac{(1+\sqrt2)^{22}+(1-\sqrt2)^{22}}{2}\\[2pt]
&=131\,836\,323 .
\end{aligned}
\]

---

\[
\boxed{131\,836\,323}
\]

Thus the team leader can pick a subset of the 42 students in **131 836 323**
different ways while respecting the “difference \(1\) or \(21\)” restriction.

