        The numbers \(1,2,\dots ,42\) can be written as two rows  

\[
\begin{array}{cccccc}
1&2&\dots &20&21\\[2pt]
22&23&\dots &41&42
\end{array}
\]

Two numbers may not be taken together if they differ by \(1\) or by \(21\).
Hence the graph of forbidden pairs is a **ladder** with 21 columns:

* a vertical edge \((i,i+21)\) for each column \(i\;(1\le i\le 21)\);
* a horizontal edge \((i,i+1)\) inside the lower row \((1\le i\le 20)\);
* a horizontal edge \((i+21,i+22)\) inside the upper row \((1\le i\le 20)\);
* one extra edge \((21,22)\) (the numbers \(21\) and \(22\) also differ by \(1\)).

Thus we have a ladder graph with an additional edge joining the lower vertex of the last column
to the upper vertex of the first column.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Transfer‑matrix description  

For each column \(i\) we may choose

* nothing  (state \(0\)),
* the lower number only (state \(L\)),
* the upper number only (state \(U\)).

The vertical edge forbids \(L\) and \(U\) together, so the three states are the only possible ones.

Horizontal edges give the following admissible transitions from column \(i\) to column \(i+1\):

\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
      &0&L&U\\\hline
0&\checkmark&\checkmark&\checkmark\\
L&\checkmark&\times&\checkmark\\
U&\checkmark&\checkmark&\times
\end{array}
\]

Hence the transfer matrix is  

\[
M=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&1\\[2pt]
1&0&1\\[2pt]
1&1&0
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\text{rows}= \text{previous state},\;
\text{columns}= \text{next state}.
\]

If we ignore the extra edge \((21,22)\), the number of admissible sequences of length
\(n\) (i.e. independent sets of the ordinary ladder with \(n\) columns) is  

\[
\mathbf{1}^{\!\!T}M^{\,n-1}\mathbf{1},
\]
where \(\mathbf{1}=(1,1,1)^{T}\).

For \(n=21\)

\[
M^{20}=
\begin{pmatrix}
22619537&15994428&15994428\\
15994428&11309769&11309768\\
15994428&11309768&11309769
\end{pmatrix},
\]

and the total is  

\[
\sum_{i,j}(M^{20})_{ij}=131\,836\,323 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Removing the forbidden pair \((21,22)\)

The extra edge forbids the simultaneous choice of the lower vertex of the last column
(state \(L\) at column \(21\)) and the upper vertex of the first column (state \(U\) at column \(1\)).
All other constraints are already encoded in \(M\).

The number of sequences that have state \(U\) at column 1 and state \(L\) at column 21 is the
\((U,L)\) entry of \(M^{20}\):

\[
(M^{20})_{U,L}=11309768 .
\]

Therefore the required number of independent sets (i.e. admissible subsets of \(\{1,\dots ,42\}\)) is  

\[
131\,836\,323-11\,309\,768 = 120\,526\,555 .
\]

