        The condition “no two chosen numbers differ by 1 or 21’’ means that the numbers  

\[
\{1,2,\dots ,42\}
\]

can be arranged as a **ladder graph** with two rows of 21 vertices:

\[
\begin{array}{cccccc}
1 & 2 & \dots & 20 & 21\\[2pt]
22&23&\dots &41 &42
\end{array}
\]

* a horizontal edge joins consecutive numbers in the same row (difference 1);
* a vertical edge joins the two numbers in the same column (difference 21).

Thus we must count the independent sets of the ladder graph \(L_{21}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Column‑by‑column recurrence  

For each column \(i\;(1\le i\le 21)\) let  

* \(0\) – none of the two vertices of the column is chosen,
* \(T\) – the top vertex (number \(i\)) is chosen,
* \(B\) – the bottom vertex (number \(i+21\)) is chosen.

The vertical edge forbids the state “both”, so each column has exactly three
states.

Two consecutive columns cannot both contain the same row, i.e.

\[
\begin{aligned}
0 &\to 0,\,T,\,B,\\
T &\to 0,\,B,\\
B &\to 0,\,T .
\end{aligned}
\]

Let  

\[
x_n=\#\text{ways ending with state }0\text{ in column }n,\qquad
y_n=\#\text{ways ending with state }T,\qquad
z_n=\#\text{ways ending with state }B .
\]

From the transition rules

\[
\begin{cases}
x_n = x_{n-1}+y_{n-1}+z_{n-1},\\[2pt]
y_n = x_{n-1}+z_{n-1},\\[2pt]
z_n = x_{n-1}+y_{n-1}.
\end{cases}
\]

Because of symmetry \(y_n=z_n\); denote \(u_n=y_n=z_n\).  
Then

\[
\boxed{\;x_n = x_{n-1}+2u_{n-1}},\qquad
\boxed{\;u_n = x_{n-1}+u_{n-1}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reduction to a single recurrence  

From the second equation \(x_{n-1}=u_n-u_{n-1}\).  
Substituting in the first gives  

\[
x_n = (u_n-u_{n-1})+2u_{n-1}=u_n+u_{n-1}.
\]

Now eliminate \(x_n\) from the second equation for the next index:

\[
u_{n+1}=x_n+u_n=(u_n+u_{n-1})+u_n = 2u_n+u_{n-1}.
\]

Hence the numbers \(u_n\) satisfy the linear recurrence  

\[
\boxed{u_{n+1}=2u_n+u_{\,n-1}},\qquad u_1=1,\;u_2=2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Pell numbers  

The recurrence above is exactly that of the **Pell numbers** \(P_n\):
\[
P_0=0,\;P_1=1,\qquad P_{n}=2P_{n-1}+P_{n-2}\;(n\ge 2).
\]

Thus \(u_n=P_n\) for all \(n\ge 1\).

From \(x_n = u_n+u_{n-1}=P_n+P_{n-1}\).

The total number of independent sets for a ladder of length \(n\) is  

\[
a_n = x_n+2u_n = (P_n+P_{n-1})+2P_n = 3P_n+P_{n-1}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Evaluate for \(n=21\)

Compute Pell numbers up to \(P_{21}\):

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
n & P_n \\ \hline
0&0\\
1&1\\
2&2\\
3&5\\
4&12\\
5&29\\
6&70\\
7&169\\
8&408\\
9&985\\
10&2378\\
11&5741\\
