16&470832\\
17&1\,136\,689\\
18&2\,744\,210\\
19&6\,625\,109\\
20&15\,994\,428\\
21&38\,613\,965
\end{array}
\]

Finally  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{21}&=3P_{21}+P_{20}\\
      &=3\cdot 38\,613\,965 + 15\,994\,428\\
      &=115\,841\,895 + 15\,994\,428\\
      &=\boxed{131\,836\,323}.
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Interpretation  

Each independent set corresponds to a subset of the 42 students in which
no two chosen numbers differ by \(1\) (adjacent in the same row) or by
\(21\) (the two numbers in the same column).  
Hence the team leader can pick a subset in **131 836 323** different ways
(including the empty set).

