        The two rules never decrease the number of black squares.  
Hence a configuration can be “stuck’’ only when **no** \(2\times2\) square contains
exactly two or three black squares.  

---

### 1.  Characterising stuck configurations  

Let a \(2\times2\) block contain the four cells  

\[
\begin{matrix}
a&b\\[2pt]
c&d
\end{matrix}\qquad a,b,c,d\in\{0,1\}.
\]

If the block has 2 or 3 black cells we could apply a rule, so a stuck board
must have each block with sum \(0,1\) or \(4\).  
If a block contained two black cells, they would be at Chebyshev distance
\(1\) (horizontal, vertical or diagonal).  
Thus a stuck board cannot have two black cells at Chebyshev distance \(1\).

Consequently the black squares of a stuck board form an **independent set**
in the king’s graph (the graph whose vertices are the squares and whose
edges join squares that are a king’s move apart).  
Conversely, any independent set of the king’s graph gives a board in which
every \(2\times2\) block has at most one black square, so it is stuck.

Hence the maximal size of a stuck configuration equals the independence
number \(\alpha\) of the king’s graph on a \(64\times64\) board.

---

### 2.  Maximum independent set of kings  

Place a black square on every cell whose row and column are both odd:

\[
(1,1),(1,3),\dots ,(1,63),\;(3,1),(3,3),\dots ,(63,63).
\]

Any two such squares differ by at least two rows **and** two columns, so they
are never a king’s move apart.  
Every \(2\times2\) block contains exactly one of these squares, therefore
the configuration is stuck.

The number of such squares is  

\[
\Bigl\lceil\frac{64}{2}\Bigr\rceil\!\times\!
\Bigl\lceil\frac{64}{2}\Bigr\rceil = 32\cdot 32 = 1024 .
\]

It is well‑known (and easy to prove) that for an \(n\times n\) board the
king‑graph independence number is \(\lceil n/2\rceil^{2}\); thus **1024** is the
largest possible size of a stuck configuration.

---

### 3.  From any larger configuration we can finish the infection  

Take any board with \(k>1024\) black squares.  
Because the independence number is \(1024\), the black squares cannot be an
independent set; therefore there exists a pair of black squares at Chebyshev
distance \(1\).  Hence some \(2\times2\) block contains at least two black
squares.

* If a block contains three black squares, rule 1 turns the remaining white
square black, increasing the total count.
* If every such block contains exactly two black squares, pick one of them.
  Rule 2 flips the whole block, preserving the total number of black squares
  but moving the two black squares to the opposite corners.  
  This move changes the pattern of black squares; after finitely many such
  flips we must create a block with three black squares (otherwise we would
  obtain a cycle of configurations all having only 0, 1, 2 or 4 blacks per
  block, which would be an independent set – impossible because we started
  with more than \(1024\) blacks).

Thus from any configuration with more than \(1024\) black squares we can,
by a finite sequence of the allowed moves, produce a \(3\)-black block and
then repeatedly apply rule 1 until every square is black.

---

### 4.  Minimal guaranteed number  

There exists a stuck configuration with exactly \(1024\) black squares (the
odd‑odd pattern), so with \(k=1024\) we cannot guarantee full infection.
With \(k=1025\) the argument above shows that every initial placement can be
grown to the whole board.

\[
\boxed{\,k_{\min}=1025\,}
\]

