        Let  

* a cell be **black** (infected) = 1, **white** (sterile) = 0;  
* a board be an \(n\times n\) binary matrix \((n=64)\).

The two allowed moves are  

* **A** – a \(2\times2\) block with exactly three 1’s: change the remaining 0 to 1  
  (the total number of 1’s increases by 1);
* **B** – a \(2\times2\) block with exactly two 1’s: toggle all four cells  
  (the total number of 1’s stays the same).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A useful invariant  

Call a board **good** if every \(2\times2\) block contains at most two black squares.  
If a board is good, a move of type **B** can be performed only on a block that already
contains exactly two black squares, and after toggling the block we again obtain a block
with exactly two black squares. Hence **move B never creates a block with three black
squares**. Consequently a good board can never become a board on which move A is
applicable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A maximal good board  

Consider the board  

\[
\begin{array}{c}
\underbrace{\color{black}{\blacksquare\;\blacksquare\;\dots\;\blacksquare}}_{\text{row }1}
\\[-2mm]
\underbrace{\color{black}{\blacksquare}\;\underbrace{\color{white}{\square\;\square\;\dots\;\square}}_{\text{columns }2\ldots n}
\\[-2mm]
\vdots\\[-2mm]
\underbrace{\color{black}{\blacksquare}\;\underbrace{\color{white}{\square\;\square\;\dots\;\square}}_{\text{columns }2\ldots n}
\end{array}
\]

i.e. the whole first row and the whole first column are black **except the
corner \((1,1)\)**, which is white.  
The board contains  

\[
( n-1 ) + ( n-1 ) = 2n-2
\]

black squares.  
Every \(2\times2\) block either lies completely outside the first row and column
(and therefore has at most one black square) or it contains the corner \((1,1)\);
in the latter case the block has exactly the two black squares \((1,2)\) and \((2,1)\).
Hence the board is good.  
Because a good board can never acquire a three‑black block, move A is never
available, and move B only permutes the existing black squares.  
Thus **any board with \(2n-2\) black squares may be stuck**, i.e. it need not be
possible to turn the whole board black.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The extremal number  

Let \(f(n)\) be the largest possible number of black squares in an \(n\times n\)
board that contains **no** \(2\times2\) block with three black squares.
The board constructed above shows \(f(n)\ge 2n-2\).

Conversely, suppose a board has more than \(2n-2\) black squares.
Count the black squares row‑wise.  Because the total exceeds \(2n-2\),
by the pigeon‑hole principle there are two rows, say \(r\) and \(s\), whose
combined number of black squares is at least \(n+1\).  In the \(n\) columns,
by the same argument there are two columns, say \(c\) and \(d\), whose
combined number of black squares is at least \(n+1\).
Among the four cells \((r,c),(r,d),(s,c),(s,d)\) at least three must be black,
otherwise the total number of black squares in those two rows and two columns
would be at most \(2n-2\), contradicting the assumption.
Hence a \(2\times2\) block with three black squares **must exist**.

Therefore  

\[
f(n)=2n-2 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  From \(2n-1\) blacks we can always finish  

If a board contains at least \(2n-1\) black squares, it necessarily has a
\(2\times2\) block with three black squares, so move A can be performed.
After one application of move A the number of black squares increases by 1,
so the board now has at least \(2n\) black squares, which is still \(>f(n)\);
hence it again contains a three‑black block.
Repeating this argument we can keep applying move A until all
\(n^{2}\) squares are black.  Thus **every board with at least \(2n-1\) black
squares can be turned completely black**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Minimal guaranteed number  

The construction of §2 shows that \(k=2n-2\) does **not** guarantee success,
while the argument of §4 shows that \(k=2n-1\) **does** guarantee success.
Hence the smallest such \(k\) is  

\[
k_{\min}=2n-1 .
\]

For the given \(64\times64\) petri dish (\(n=64\))

\[
\boxed{\,k_{\min}=2\cdot64-1 = 127\,}.
\]

