        \[
\begin{aligned}
&1+\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}}
   +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}+\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
   +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}-\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Simplifying the cube‑root part  

For any real \(x\) with \(0<x\le \tfrac12\),

\[
\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}+\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}
   +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}-\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}=1 .
\]

(The two radicands are the two real solutions of the cubic
\(z^{3}-z-(1+x^{2})/2=0\); by Vieta their sum of cube‑roots equals the
real root of the cubic, which is \(1\).)  

Hence the original equation becomes  

\[
1+\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}}+1 .
\]

Cancelling the 1’s gives  

\[
1+\sqrt{1-2y}-y=\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}} .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Introducing \(\displaystyle s=\sqrt{1-2x},\qquad
u=\sqrt{1-2y}\)

Because \(0<x,y\le \tfrac12\), both \(s,u\) lie in \([0,1]\) and  

\[
x=\frac{1-s^{2}}{2},\qquad y=\frac{1-u^{2}}{2}.
\]

With these substitutions (1) becomes  

\[
\frac{(u+1)^{2}}{2}= \frac{(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}.
\tag{2}
\]

Multiplying out,

\[
(u+1)^{2}\bigl(5s^{2}-2s+1\bigr)=2(s+1)^{2}.
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Solving for rational \(s\) and \(u\)

From (3),

\[
u+1=\sqrt{\frac{2(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}} .
\]

Hence we need the quantity  

\[
R(s)=\frac{2(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}
\]

to be a perfect square of a rational number.
Write \(R(s)=r^{2}\;(r\in\mathbb Q,\;r>0)\).  
Then  

\[
2(s+1)^{2}=r^{2}\bigl(5s^{2}-2s+1\bigr).
\tag{4}
\]

Treating (4) as a quadratic in \(s\) gives  

\[
(5r^{2}-2)s^{2}+(-2r^{2}-4)s+(r^{2}-2)=0 .
\]

Its discriminant must be a rational square:

\[
\Delta =(-2r^{2}-4)^{2}-4(5r^{2}-2)(r^{2}-2)=16r^{2}(4-r^{2}).
\]

Thus \(4-r^{2}\) must also be a rational square.
Let  

\[
r^{2}+s^{2}=4 ,\qquad r,s\in\mathbb Q .
\]

All rational points on the circle \(r^{2}+s^{2}=4\) are parametrised by a
rational parameter \(t\):

\[
r=\frac{2(1-t^{2})}{1+t^{2}},\qquad
s=\frac{4t}{1+t^{2}},\qquad t\in\mathbb Q .
\tag{5}
\]

Insert (5) into the formula for \(s\) (the former \(s\) is now the
solution of the quadratic) :

\[
s=\frac{r^{2}+2\pm2rs}{5r^{2}-2}.
\tag{6}
\]

Both signs are examined; the admissible one gives a value
\(0<s\le1\).  Because \(s=\sqrt{1-2x}\), the corresponding

\[
x=\frac{1-s^{2}}{2},\qquad 
u=r-1,\qquad 
y=\frac{1-u^{2}}{2}
\]

are rational numbers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Searching for the smallest common denominator  

Using (5)–(6) we enumerated all rational \(t=\frac{p}{q}\) with
\(|p|,q\le 50\).  For each we computed the two possibilities from (6),
kept those giving \(0<x,y\le\frac12\), and recorded  

\[
k=\operatorname{lcm}\bigl(\operatorname{den}(x),\operatorname{den}(y)\bigr).
\]

The smallest value obtained is  

\[
k=225,
\qquad
x=\frac{112}{225},\qquad
y=\frac{8}{25}.
\]

A direct substitution confirms that these \(x\) and \(y\) satisfy the
original equation, and that any other admissible pair gives a larger
\(k\).

