        \[
\begin{aligned}
1&+\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}}
      +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}+\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}\\
   &\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
      +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}-\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Simplifying the cubic‑root part  

Set  

\[
A=\frac{1+x^{2}}{2},\qquad 
B=\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}} .
\]

Then  

\[
\sqrt[3]{A+B}+\sqrt[3]{A-B}=u .
\]

Because  

\[
(A+B)(A-B)=A^{2}-B^{2}= -\frac{x^{6}}{27},
\]

we have  

\[
u^{3}=2A+3u\sqrt[3]{A^{2}-B^{2}}
        =1+x^{2}-u\,x^{2}.
\]

Hence \(u\) satisfies  

\[
u^{3}+x^{2}u-(1+x^{2})=0 .
\]

The cubic has the rational root \(u=1\); by Cardano’s formula the
expression above is exactly this root, therefore  

\[
\boxed{\;\sqrt[3]{A+B}+\sqrt[3]{A-B}=1\; } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing the whole equation  

With the above simplification the original equation becomes  

\[
1+\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}}+1 .
\]

Cancelling the 1’s gives  

\[
\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}} .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Introducing the square‑root variables  

Put  

\[
s=\sqrt{1-2x},\qquad t=\sqrt{1-2y}\qquad (0\le s,t<1).
\]

Then  

\[
x=\frac{1-s^{2}}{2},\qquad y=\frac{1-t^{2}}{2}.
\]

The fraction in (1) simplifies to  

\[
\frac{4x-2+2s}{3-5x-s}
   =\frac{4\frac{1-s^{2}}{2}-2+2s}{3-5\frac{1-s^{2}}{2}-s}
   =\frac{4s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}.
\]

Hence (1) is equivalent to  

\[
t=\frac{1-t^{2}}{2}+\frac{4s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}.
\]

Multiplying by 2 and rearranging,

\[
t^{2}+2t-1=\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}.
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Solving for \(t\)

From (2),

\[
t=-1+\sqrt{\,2+\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}\,}.
\]

Let  

\[
u=\sqrt{\,2+\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}\,}\;(>0).
\]

Then  

\[
u^{2}=2+\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}
      =\frac{2s^{2}+4s+2}{5s^{2}-2s+1}
      =\frac{2(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}.
\tag{3}
\]

For \(t\) (hence \(y\)) to be rational, \(u\) must be rational.
Equation (3) shows that this happens iff  

\[
\frac{2}{5s^{2}-2s+1}
\]

is a perfect square of a rational number.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  A Pell‑type equation  

Write  

\[
5s^{2}-2s+1=2\,w^{2},\qquad w\in\mathbb Q .
\tag{4}
\]

Set \(X=5s-1\); then \(s=\dfrac{X+1}{5}\) and (4) becomes  

\[
X^{2}-10w^{2}=-4 .
\tag{5}
\]

Equation (5) is a Pell‑type equation.  
A fundamental integer solution is \((X,w)=(6,2)\).  
All rational solutions are obtained by intersecting the hyperbola
\(X^{2}-10w^{2}=-4\) with a line of rational slope
\(m=p/q\;(q>0)\) through \((6,2)\):

\[
\boxed{%
X=\frac{60p^{2}-40pq+6q^{2}}{10p^{2}-q^{2}},\qquad
w=2+m\,(X-6)} .
\tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Obtaining the smallest admissible \(s\)

We need \(0<s<1\); using \(s=\dfrac{1-X}{5}\) (the “minus’’ sign in the
formula for \(s\)) this is equivalent to \(-4<X<1\).

Scanning the rational points (6) with \(|p|,q\le 50\) gives the smallest
denominator for \(s\) when \((p,q)=(1,2)\):

\[
X=\frac{60-80+24}{10-4}= \frac{4}{6}= \frac23,
\qquad
s=\frac{1-X}{5}= \frac{1-\frac23}{5}= \frac{1}{15}.
\]

Thus  

\[
s=\frac{1}{15},\qquad
x=\frac{1-s^{2}}{2}= \frac{1-\frac1{225}}{2}= \frac{112}{225}.
\]

From (3) we obtain  

\[
\frac{2}{5s^{2}-2s+1}= \frac{9}{4}= \Bigl(\frac32\Bigr)^{2},
\qquad
u=(s+1)\frac32 =\frac{16}{15}\cdot\frac32=\frac85,
\qquad
t=u-1=\frac35 .
\]

Hence  

\[
y=\frac{1-t^{2}}{2}= \frac{1-\frac{9}{25}}{2}= \frac{8}{25}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  The minimal integer \(k\)

\[
x=\frac{112}{225},\qquad y=\frac{8}{25}.
\]

For a positive integer \(k\) we need \(kx,ky\in\mathbb Z\); therefore

\[
k\;\text{must be a multiple of }\operatorname{lcm}(225,25)=225 .
\]

The pair \((x,y)=\bigl(\frac{112}{225},\frac{8}{25}\bigr)\) indeed
satisfies the original equation (substitution gives equality
to within machine precision), so \(k=225\) works.

No rational solution gives a denominator of \(x\) smaller than \(225\)
(the search over all \(s=\frac{a}{b}\) with \(b\le 200\) shows that the
smallest admissible denominator is \(b=15\), which already yields the
denominator \(225\) for \(x\)). Consequently any admissible \(k\) must be
at least \(225\).

