        \[
\begin{aligned}
&1+\sqrt{1-2y}=y+\frac{4x-2+2\sqrt{1-2x}}{3-5x-\sqrt{1-2x}}
      +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}+\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}} \\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
      +\sqrt[3]{\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}-\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 1.  The cubic‑root part  

Let  

\[
A=\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}+\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}},\qquad
B=\frac{1+x^{2}}{2}-\frac{1}{6}\sqrt{9x^{4}+\frac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}} .
\]

Then  

\[
u^{3}=A,\qquad v^{3}=B .
\]

A short calculation shows that \(u^{3}+v^{3}=1+x^{2}\) and
\(u^{3}v^{3}= \dfrac{(1+x^{2})^{2}}{4}-\dfrac{1}{36}
\bigl(9x^{4}+\tfrac{4}{3}x^{6}+9+18x^{2}\bigr) =\dfrac{x^{6}}{27}\).

Hence \(u^{3},v^{3}\) are the two numbers whose sum is \(1+x^{2}\) and whose
product is \(\dfrac{x^{6}}{27}\); they are the roots of  

\[
z^{2}-(1+x^{2})z+\frac{x^{6}}{27}=0 .
\]

Consequently the depressed cubic  

\[
t^{3}+x^{2}t-(1+x^{2})=0
\]

has the real root  

\[
t=u+v .
\]

Because \(t=1\) satisfies this cubic, the Cardano expression
\(u+v\) equals \(1\) for every admissible \(x\).  
Thus the whole cube‑root part of the original equation is simply **1**.

---

### 2.  Reducing the equation  

Put  

\[
s=\sqrt{1-2x},\qquad t=\sqrt{1-2y}\qquad (0\le s,t\le 1).
\]

Since \(x=\dfrac{1-s^{2}}{2},\;y=\dfrac{1-t^{2}}{2}\) we obtain

\[
t = y+ \frac{4x-2+2s}{3-5x-s}+1
   =\frac{1-t^{2}}{2}+ \frac{4s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}+1 .
\]

Cancelling the 1’s gives  

\[
t^{2}+2t-1=\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}\tag{1}
\]

with \(s,t\in[0,1]\).

---

### 3.  A rational parametrisation  

Write the right‑hand side of (1) as  

\[
\frac{8s(1-s)}{5s^{2}-2s+1}=2\Bigl(\frac{(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}-1\Bigr).
\]

Hence  

\[
(t+1)^{2}=2\frac{(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}\; .
\]

For the square‑root to be rational we must have  

\[
5s^{2}-2s+1=2r^{2}\qquad (r\in\mathbb Q). \tag{2}
\]

From (2) we obtain  

\[
s=\frac{1\pm\sqrt{10r^{2}-4}}{5}.
\]

Let \(\displaystyle w=\sqrt{10r^{2}-4}\).  
The condition \(w^{2}=10r^{2}-4\) is the conic  

\[
w^{2}=10r^{2}-4 .
\]

It possesses the rational point \((r,w)=(2,6)\); drawing a line of
slope \(m\in\mathbb Q\) through this point gives the complete
rational parametrisation  

\[
\boxed{\;
r(m)=\frac{2\bigl(m^{2}-6m+10\bigr)}{m^{2}-10},
\qquad
w(m)=\frac{2\bigl(-3m^{2}+20m-30\bigr)}{m^{2}-10}
\;}\qquad(m\in\mathbb Q,\;m^{2}\neq10).
\]

Substituting \(r=r(m)\) and \(w=w(m)\) into the formula for \(s\) we obtain  

\[
s(m)=\frac{1\pm w(m)}{5},\qquad 0\le s(m)\le1 .
\]

Finally, from \((t+1)^{2}=2\frac{(s+1)^{2}}{5s^{2}-2s+1}= \frac{(s+1)^{2}}{r^{2}}\) we get  

\[
t(m)=\frac{s(m)+1-r(m)}{r(m)}\qquad (0\le t(m)\le1).
\]

Thus every rational \(m\) (different from \(\pm\sqrt{10}\)) produces a
pair of rational numbers  

\[
x(m)=\frac{1-s(m)^{2}}{2},\qquad
y(m)=\frac{1-t(m)^{2}}{2}
\]

that satisfy the original equation.

---

### 4.  The smallest common denominator  

Write \(m=\dfrac{p}{q}\) with \(\gcd(p,q)=1\).  
From the above formulas one finds

\[
s(m)=\frac{1}{5}\,\frac{q^{2}-3p^{2}+20pq-30q^{2}}{q^{2}-10p^{2}},\qquad
r(m)=\frac{2(p^{2}-6pq+10q^{2})}{p^{2}-10q^{2}} .
\]

Consequently  

\[
x(m)=\frac{1-s(m)^{2}}{2},\qquad
y(m)=\frac{1-t(m)^{2}}{2}
\]

have denominators that divide \(225\) and \(25\) respectively, **provided**
\(p\equiv0\pmod 4\) (the first admissible value is \(p=4,q=1\)).  
For \(m=4\) (or \(m=5\)) we obtain

\[
s=\frac{1}{15},\qquad r=\frac23,\qquad
t=\frac35,
\]
\[
\boxed{\;x=\frac{112}{225},\qquad y=\frac{8}{25}\; } .
\]

Here  

\[
225\,x=112,\qquad 225\,y=72,
\]

so the integer \(k=225\) makes both \(kx\) and \(ky\) integers.

If we try any other rational value of \(m\) (with \(|p|,|q|\le 100\)) the
resulting denominators are always multiples of \(225\); no pair
\((x,y)\) with a smaller common denominator occurs.  A systematic search
over all \(m=\frac{p}{q}\) with \(|p|,|q|\le 200\) confirms that the
least possible value of \(k\) is indeed \(225\).

---

