        [Turn 2 REASONING] (2861 chars)
        Let  

* rows   : \(R=\{r_{1},\dots ,r_{2025}\}\)  
* columns: \(C=\{c_{1},\dots ,c_{2048}\}\)

A red square is an edge \((r_i ,c_j)\) of the bipartite graph \(G\) whose vertex‑set is
\(R\cup C\).
The condition *“at most 128 red squares in each row and each column’’* means  

\[
\deg_G(v)\le 128\qquad\text{for every }v\in R\cup C .
\]

A **diagonal** is a set of 2025 squares, one from each row and from distinct
columns; i.e. a *perfect matching* of the rows to a subset of the columns.
Thus a diagonal corresponds to a perfect matching of the bipartite graph
\(K_{2025,2048}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Upper bound – 128 diagonals always suffice  

The maximum degree of \(G\) is \(\Delta\le 128\).  
For a bipartite graph König’s line‑colouring theorem gives an edge‑colouring
with exactly \(\Delta\) colours, each colour class being a matching.
Hence the red edges can be coloured with at most \(128\) colours so that
no two edges of the same colour share a row or a column.

Fix one colour, say colour \(k\).
The edges of colour \(k\) form a matching \(M_k\); each row appears in
\(M_k\) at most once and each column at most once.
If a row is not incident to an edge of colour \(k\) we may join it to any
column that is not already used by \(M_k\) (there are at least
\(2048-2025=23\) columns left unused).  
Because every unused row is adjacent to all unused columns, Hall’s theorem
guarantees a perfect matching extending \(M_k\) to a set of 2025 pairwise
different columns.  This extended matching is a diagonal that contains all
red squares of colour \(k\).

Doing this for each of the at most 128 colour classes we obtain at most
\(128\) diagonals that together cover every red square. Consequently  

\[
n\le 128 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Lower bound – 128 diagonals may be necessary  

Place exactly \(128\) red squares in every row, and arrange them so that
every column also contains at most \(128\) red squares (this is possible,
e.g. by a cyclic shift of the columns).  
Then the total number of red squares is  

\[
|E(G)| = 2025\cdot 128 = 259\,200 .
\]

A diagonal contains at most one square from each row, therefore it can
cover at most \(2025\) red squares.  Hence any family of diagonals covering
all red squares must satisfy  

\[
\#\text{diagonals}\;\ge\;
\Big\lceil\frac{259\,200}{2025}\Big\rceil
= \Big\lceil 128\Big\rceil =128 .
\]

Thus \(n\ge 128\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimum value

From the upper bound and the lower bound we obtain

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\min}=128\,}.
\]

So, no matter how the red squares are placed (with at most 128 per row and
per column), we can always cover them with 128 diagonals, and in some
placements 128 diagonals are indeed necessary.

