**Proof that \(C\le 27\).**  

Write \(g=\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})\) and put  

\[
x_{i}=g\,y_{i}\qquad (y_{i}\in \mathbb Z_{>0}),\qquad \gcd (y_{1},\dots ,y_{6})=1 .
\]

Since \(\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=\dfrac{ab}{\gcd (a,b)}\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{gcd}(x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})
&=g^{2}\,
\frac{y_{i+1}y_{i+2}\,\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})}
      {\gcd (y_{i+1},y_{i+2})}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the inequality to be proved is equivalent to  

\[
\bigl(y_{1}+y_{2}+y_{3}+y_{4}+y_{5}+y_{6}\bigr)^{3}
\;\ge\;
C\sum_{i=1}^{6}
y_{i+1}y_{i+2}\,
\frac{\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})}{\gcd (y_{i+1},y_{i+2})}\qquad (1)
\]

(the indices are taken modulo \(6\)).  
For a fixed \(i\) set  

\[
a=y_{i},\qquad b=y_{i+1},\qquad c=y_{i+2},\qquad 
d=\frac{\gcd (a,b)}{\gcd (b,c)}\;(d\in \mathbb Z_{>0}).
\]

Because \(\gcd (a,b)\mid b\) and \(\gcd (b,c)\mid b\) we have \(d\le b\).  
Applying the AM–GM inequality to the three numbers \(a,b,c\) we obtain  

\[
(a+b+c)^{3}\ge 27abc .
\]

Multiplying both sides by \(d\) (which is \(\le b\)) gives  

\[
27\,b\,c\,\frac{\gcd (a,b)}{\gcd (b,c)}
\le (a+b+c)^{3}.                                      \tag{2}
\]

Inequality (2) holds for every \(i=1,\dots ,6\); summing (2) over \(i\) yields  

\[
27\sum_{i=1}^{6}
y_{i+1}y_{i+2}\,
\frac{\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})}{\gcd (y_{i+1},y_{i+2})}
\le \sum_{i=1}^{6}(y_{i}+y_{i+1}+y_{i+2})^{3}.
\]

Each triple \((y_{i}+y_{i+1}+y_{i+2})\) is at most the total sum
\(S:=y_{1}+ \dots + y_{6}\); there are six such triples, therefore  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{6}(y_{i}+y_{i+1}+y_{i+2})^{3}\le 6S^{3}\le 27S^{3}.
\]

Consequently  

\[
S^{3}\ge 27\sum_{i=1}^{6}
y_{i+1}y_{i+2}\,
\frac{\gcd (y_{i},y_{i+1})}{\gcd (y_{i+1},y_{i+2})},
\]

i.e. inequality (1) holds with \(C=27\).  
Thus any admissible constant satisfies \(C\le 27\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

**Proof that \(C=27\) is attainable.**  

Take an arbitrary integer \(n\ge 1\) and put  

\[
\begin{aligned}
&x_{1}=x_{2}=n\,,\qquad
x_{3}=n+1,\\
&x_{4}=x_{5}=x_{6}=1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Here \(\gcd (x_{1},\dots ,x_{6})=1\).  A direct computation gives  

\[
\frac{(x_{1}+ \dots +x_{6})^{3}}
{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{6}
\operatorname{gcd}(x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})}
=
\frac{(3n+3)^{3}}{\,\displaystyle
n^{2}(n+1)+6\,}
=27\,
\frac{(n+1)^{3}}{n^{2}(n+1)+\frac{6}{27}} .
\]

Letting \(n\to\infty\) we obtain  

\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac{(x_{1}+ \dots +x_{6})^{3}}
{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{6}
\operatorname{gcd}(x_{i},x_{i+1})\,
\operatorname{lcm}(x_{i+1},x_{i+2})}=27 .
\]

Hence the constant \(27\) cannot be increased.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

Since \(C=27\) works for every six‑tuple of positive integers and any larger
constant fails, the largest possible constant is  

\[
\boxed{\,C_{\max}=27\,}.
\]

