        Let  

\[
L(T)=\min\{\text{number of lines that cover all points of }T\}
\]

for a finite set \(T\) of points in the plane.  
Condition  

* i) \(L(S)>2012\) (there is **no** family of 2012 lines covering \(S\)), and there is no circle through all points of \(S\).

* ii) For every \(X\in S\) we have \(L(S\setminus\{X\})\le 2012\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A simple lower bound for \(L(T)\)

If no three points of \(T\) are collinear, each line can contain at most two points of \(T\).  
Hence at least  

\[
\Big\lceil\frac{|T|}{2}\Big\rceil
\]

lines are necessary, and this bound is attainable by pairing the points arbitrarily.  
Consequently, for any set \(T\)

\[
\Big\lceil\frac{|T|}{2}\Big\rceil\le L(T)\le |T|.
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Upper bound for \(|S|\)

From (ii) we must have  

\[
L(S\setminus\{X\})\le 2012\qquad\forall X\in S .
\]

Applying (1) to the \((|S|-1)\)-point set \(S\setminus\{X\}\),

\[
\Big\lceil\frac{|S|-1}{2}\Big\rceil\le 2012 .
\]

Thus  

\[
|S|-1\le 4024\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad |S|\le 4025 .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Lower bound for \(|S|\)

Condition (i) requires \(L(S)>2012\).  Using (1) again,

\[
\Big\lceil\frac{|S|}{2}\Big\rceil>2012\quad\Longrightarrow\quad |S|>4024 .
\]

Hence  

\[
|S|\ge 4025 .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Determination of the maximum

From (2) and (3) we obtain the only possible size

\[
\boxed{|S|=4025}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Existence of a set with \(|S|=4025\)

Take the 4025 points  

\[
P_i=(i,i^{2})\qquad i=1,2,\dots ,4025 .
\]

* No three of them are collinear (a parabola contains at most two points of a line).  
  Hence the minimum number of lines needed to cover the whole set is  

  \[
  L(S)=\Big\lceil\frac{4025}{2}\Big\rceil=2013>2012 .
  \]

* After removing any point we have 4024 points, which can be paired and covered by exactly  

  \[
  L(S\setminus\{P_i\})=\frac{4024}{2}=2012
  \]

  lines.  Thus condition (ii) holds.

* The points lie on a parabola, not on a single circle; therefore there is no circle passing through all of them, satisfying the second part of (i).

Hence a set of 4025 points satisfying both conditions exists, and no larger set can satisfy them.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Maximum possible cardinality of \(S\) is \(\displaystyle 4025\).**

