        Let  

\[
\chi(S)=\text{the smallest number of straight lines that cover all points of }S .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determining the covering number  

* (i) No 2012 lines cover \(S\); therefore \(\chi(S)>2012\).
* (ii) For every \(X\in S\) the set \(S\setminus\{X\}\) **is** coverable by 2012 lines, i.e.  
  \(\chi(S\setminus\{X\})\le 2012\).

If we add the point \(X\) back, we can cover it by one extra line, so  

\[
\chi(S)\le \chi(S\setminus\{X\})+1\le 2013 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
\boxed{\chi(S)=2013 } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  An upper bound for \(|S|\)

Take a minimal covering of \(S\) by the 2013 lines  
\(\mathcal L=\{L_1,\dots ,L_{2013}\}\).

*If a line \(L_i\) contained no point that is **only** on \(L_i\), then \(L_i\) could be omitted, contradicting the minimality.*  
Hence each line \(L_i\) possesses at least one **private point** \(P_i\) that lies on no other line of \(\mathcal L\).

All remaining points of \(S\) must lie on at least two of the lines of \(\mathcal L\); otherwise they would also be private.  
Thus every non‑private point is incident with a pair of distinct lines \((L_i,L_j)\).  
Two different pairs of lines cannot give the same point, because two distinct lines intersect in at most one point (they are not parallel in an extremal configuration).  

Therefore

\[
|S|\le \underbrace{2013}_{\text{private points}}+
        \underbrace{\binom{2013}{2}}_{\text{points belonging to two lines}}
      =\frac{2013\,(2013+1)}{2}=2013\cdot1007=2\,027\,091 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  

Choose 2013 lines in *general position* (no two parallel, no three concurrent).  

* On each line \(L_i\) pick a point \(P_i\) that is not an intersection of any two lines.  
  These are the private points.
* Add **all** intersection points of the 2013 lines.

The obtained set  

\[
S=\{P_1,\dots ,P_{2013}\}\cup\{L_i\cap L_j\mid 1\le i<j\le2013\}
\]

has exactly  

\[
2013+\binom{2013}{2}=2\,027\,091
\]

points.

* No 2012 lines can cover \(S\): if a line is omitted, the intersection of the two omitted lines is uncovered.
* Removing any point:
  * If a private point \(P_i\) is removed, the line \(L_i\) becomes unnecessary; the remaining 2012 lines cover the rest.
  * If an intersection point \(L_i\cap L_j\) is removed, replace the two lines \(L_i,L_j\) by the single line \(P_iP_j\); together with the other 2011 lines we again have 2012 covering lines.
* By a generic choice of the lines the points are not all concyclic, so the circle condition is also satisfied.

Thus the bound is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle |S|_{\max}=2\,027\,091}
\]

