        **Solution Overview**

Let the three classes be \(A,B,C\) with sizes  

\[
|A|=a,\qquad |B|=b,\qquad |C|=c .
\]

For a student \(x\) denote by  

* \(d_{x}^{C}=|\,\text{students of }C\text{ known by }x\,|\) – the degree from \(x\) to the third class.  

The condition in the problem says that for any two students from different classes,
their neighbour‑sets in the third class intersect in exactly \(10\) students and also
have exactly \(10\) students that are adjacent to **neither** of them.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Regularity of the bipartite graphs  

Take \(a\in A,\;b\in B\).  
Let  

\[
\alpha = d_{a}^{C},\qquad \alpha' = d_{b}^{C}.
\]

From the statement

\[
|N_C(a)\cap N_C(b)| =10,\qquad
|C\setminus(N_C(a)\cup N_C(b))| =10 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\alpha+\alpha' -10 =|N_C(a)\cup N_C(b)|
                =c-10\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\alpha+\alpha'=c .
\]

Thus the sum of the two degrees is always \(c\); consequently all students of
\(A\) have the same degree \(\alpha\) toward \(C\) and all students of \(B\) have
the same degree \(c-\alpha\) toward \(C\).  
Denote

\[
\alpha = d_{A}^{C},\qquad
\beta = d_{A}^{B},\qquad
\gamma = d_{B}^{A}.
\]

Exactly the same reasoning for the other two pairs of classes gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{(A–C)} &&\text{rows of }A\!C\text{ have } \alpha\text{ ones, columns }a-\gamma,\\
&\text{(A–B)} &&\text{rows of }A\!B\text{ have } \beta\text{ ones, columns } \gamma,\\
&\text{(B–C)} &&\text{rows of }B\!C\text{ have } c-\alpha\text{ ones, columns } b-\beta .
\end{aligned}
\]

The three bipartite graphs are therefore **regular**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Relations between the parameters  

From the regularity we have the three degree–sum equations  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a\alpha &= c\,(a-\gamma), \tag{1}\\[2mm]
a\beta  &= b\gamma,      \tag{2}\\[2mm]
b\,(c-\alpha) &= c\,(b-\beta). \tag{3}
\end{aligned}
\]

Counting triples \((a,b,c)\) that are mutually adjacent (e.g. an \(A\)-student,
a \(B\)-student and a common neighbour in \(C\)) in two ways gives three more
identities:

\[
\begin{aligned}
10ab &= c\,(a-\gamma)(b-\beta), \tag{4}\\
10ac &= b\,\gamma\,(c-\alpha), \tag{5}\\
10bc &= a\,\beta\,\alpha. \tag{6}
\end{aligned}
\]

All variables are non‑negative integers and \(0\le\alpha\le c,\;0\le\beta\le b,\;
0\le\gamma\le a\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Solving the system  

From (1) and (3) we obtain two expressions for \(\alpha\):

\[
\alpha =\frac{c\beta}{b}=c-\frac{c\gamma}{a}.
\]

Equating them yields  

\[
\frac{c\beta}{b}=c-\frac{c\gamma}{a}
\Longrightarrow a\beta = b\,(a-\gamma).
\]

Together with (2) (\(a\beta=b\gamma\)) we get  

\[
b\gamma = b\,(a-\gamma)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \gamma=\frac{a}{2}.
\]

Hence \(a\) is even and \(\displaystyle\gamma=\frac a2\).

Using (2) now gives  

\[
a\beta = b\frac a2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \beta =\frac b2,
\]

so \(b\) is even.  
From (1) we obtain  

\[
\alpha =\frac{c\beta}{b}= \frac{c}{2},
\]

hence \(c\) is even as well.

Thus

\[
\boxed{\ \alpha=\frac c2,\qquad \beta=\frac b2,\qquad \gamma=\frac a2\ } .
\]

Insert these into (4)–(6).  
Equation (4) becomes  

\[
10ab = c\Bigl(\frac a2\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac b2\Bigr)
      =\frac{c\,ab}{4}\;\Longrightarrow\;c=40 .
\]

Equation (5) gives  

\[
10ac = b\Bigl(\frac a2\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac c2\Bigr)
      =\frac{b\,ac}{4}\;\Longrightarrow\;b=40 .
\]

Finally (6) yields  

\[
10bc = a\Bigl(\frac b2\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac c2\Bigr)
      =\frac{a\,bc}{4}\;\Longrightarrow\;a=40 .
\]

All three classes have the same size:

\[
a=b=c=40 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Verification of the conditions  

With \(a=b=c=40\) we have  

\[
\alpha=\beta=\gamma =20 .
\]

Every student knows exactly half of the students of each of the other two
classes.  
For two students from different classes each has \(20\) neighbours in the
third class; their intersection must be \(10\) (by the problem statement), and
the remaining \(20\) vertices of the third class are split equally between
“knowing exactly one of them’’ and “knowing none of them’’ – precisely the
required \(10\) that know neither.

The algebraic derivation shows that any configuration satisfying the
statement forces the three class sizes to be \(40\).  A symmetric
\(2\!-\!(40,20,10)\) design (which indeed exists, e.g. from a Paley
construction with \(v=41\) after deleting one point) provides an explicit
realisation, confirming that the numbers are attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Answer  

The total number of students in the three classes is  

\[
\boxed{40+40+40 = 120 } .
\]

