### 1.  Which integers can occur as \(r_i\) ?

For a point \((x,y)\) on  

\[
3x^{2}+3y^{2}=8+4xy+2(x+y)|x-y|
\]

write \(s=x+y,\;t=x-y\).  
The curve becomes  

\[
\begin{cases}
s^{2}-4st+5t^{2}=16 & (t\ge 0)\\[2mm]
s^{2}+4st+5t^{2}=16 & (t\le 0)
\end{cases}
\]

so \(|s|\le 4\sqrt5\;( \approx 8.944)\).  
Hence  

\[
0\le d=\frac{|s-6|}{\sqrt2}\le\frac{\,|\,4\sqrt5-6\,|}{\sqrt2}\approx 10.57 .
\]

Define  

\[
f(d)=\frac{21-15\sqrt2}{2}\Bigl(d^{3}-3d^{2}-6\sqrt2\,d^{2}+18d+6\sqrt2\,d
+\frac{28}{3}+\frac{16\sqrt2}{3}\Bigr).
\]

A short numerical check on the whole admissible interval \(0\le d\le10.57\) gives  

\[
-21\le f(d)\le 2.36 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
r_i=\lfloor f(d_i)\rfloor\in\{-21,-20,\dots ,2\}.
\]

In particular the **largest possible value is \(2\)**; the value \(3\) never occurs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing the problem to an integer‑counting problem  

Only the integers \(-2,-1,0,1,2\) can be used without violating the
square‑sum condition (any \(|r_i|\ge3\) would already contribute at least
\(9\) to \(\sum r_i^{2}\), which would leave too few squares for the
remaining \(2025\) numbers).  
Thus let  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a&=\#\{r_i=2\}, & b&=\#\{r_i=1\}, & c&=\#\{r_i=0\},\\
d&=\#\{r_i=-1\},& e&=\#\{r_i=-2\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The given data become  

\[
\begin{cases}
a+b+c+d+e =2025,\\[2mm]
2a+b-d-2e =2021,\\[2mm]
4a+b+d+4e =2043 .
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

From (1) we obtain  

\[
\begin{aligned}
b &=2032-3a-e,\\
d &= 11-a-3e,\\
c &=3a+3e-18 .
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]

All numbers must be non‑negative, giving the constraints  

\[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
&2032-3a-e\ge0,\\
&11-a-3e\ge0,\\
&3a+3e-18\ge0,\\
&a,e\ge0 .
\end{aligned}}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximising \(\displaystyle\sum r_i^{4}\)

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{2025} r_i^{4}=16a+16e+(b+d)
                     =16(a+e)+\bigl(2043-4a-4e\bigr)
                     =2043+12(a+e).
\]

Hence we have to maximise \(a+e\) under (3).

From the second inequality of (3),

\[
a+3e\le 11\quad\Longrightarrow\quad a+e\le 11-2e.
\]

Since the right‑hand side decreases when \(e\) grows, the maximum is
obtained at \(e=0\), giving \(a\le 11\) and \(a+e\le 11\).
The third inequality of (3) forces \(a+e\ge 6\), which is satisfied.

Thus the largest possible value is  

\[
a+e=11\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\sum r_i^{4}=2043+12\cdot 11 = 2175 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Realisability

The interval where \(\lfloor f(d)\rfloor =2\) is  

\[
5.528\ldots\le d<6.893\ldots .
\]

For every \(d\) in this interval the curve provides two distinct points
(\(t>0\) and \(t<0\)), so we can choose \(11\) different points with
\(r_i=2\).

The intervals for \(r_i=1\) and \(r_i=0\) are non‑empty as well,
hence we can select \(1999\) points with \(r_i=1\) and \(15\) points with
\(r_i=0\).  All \(2025\) points are distinct, satisfying the original
geometric condition.

