        The piles are  

\[
S_{1},S_{2},\dots ,S_{7671},\qquad |S_k|=k .
\]

In one operation we choose an ordered pair \((i,j)\;(i<j)\) that has not been used before,
both piles are non‑empty and we remove  

\[
d=\gcd(|S_i|,|S_j|)
\]

coins from each of the two piles.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Upper bound  

*Each operation removes at least one coin from each of the two piles*, therefore a pile
\(S_k\) can lose at most \(\min (k,\,7669)\) coins – it can be paired with at most the
\(7669\) other piles, and it cannot lose more coins than it originally contains.

Hence the total number of coins that can be removed is at most  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{7671}\min(k,7669)
   =\sum_{k=1}^{7669}k+7669
   =\frac{7669\cdot 7670}{2}+7669
   =29\,425\,955 .
\]

Every operation removes two coins, so the number \(M\) of possible operations satisfies  

\[
2M\le 29\,425\,955\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
M\le\Bigl\lfloor\frac{29\,425\,955}{2}\Bigr\rfloor=14\,712\,977 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A graph that attains the bound  

Consider the simple graph \(G\) on the vertex set \(\{1,2,\dots ,7671\}\) whose degree
sequence is  

\[
d_1=1,\;d_2=2,\; \dots ,\; d_{7670}=7670,\;
d_{7671}=7669 .
\]

The sum of the degrees is  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{7670}k+7669=29\,425\,954,
\]

which is even, and the Erdős–Gallai theorem guarantees that this sequence is
graphical; therefore such a graph \(G\) exists.  
Let each edge \(\{i,j\}\) of \(G\) correspond to the ordered pair \((i,j)\) (with
\(i<j\)).  The graph contains  

\[
\frac{29\,425\,954}{2}=14\,712\,977
\]

edges, i.e. that many admissible ordered pairs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Ordering the edges – guaranteeing \(\gcd =1\)

Start with the graph \(G\) and the piles in their initial sizes.
Repeatedly perform the following step:

*Pick a vertex whose current degree in the remaining graph is \(1\).  
Let its unique neighbour be \(v\).  The pile at the degree‑\(1\) vertex contains
exactly one coin, so \(\gcd(1,|S_v|)=1\).  Perform the operation on the
corresponding ordered pair, delete the edge, and decrease the two pile sizes by
\(1\).*

Because the degree‑\(1\) vertex has only one coin left, the operation is always
legal and removes exactly one coin from each of the two piles.  Deleting the edge
reduces the degree of the neighbour by one, so the invariant “the degree of a
vertex equals the current number of coins in its pile’’ is preserved.

A simple graph always possesses a vertex of degree \(1\) while any edge remains
(the leaf‑removal process of the Havel–Hakimi construction).  Consequently we can
delete edges one by one until no edge is left.  All edges of \(G\) are used, each
time removing precisely one coin from each of the two involved piles.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Result of the construction  

After all \(14\,712\,977\) operations have been performed, every pile
\(S_k\) with \(k\le 7670\) is empty (its degree \(k\) has been exhausted), while
\(S_{7671}\) has lost \(7669\) coins and still contains \(2\) coins.
No further operation is possible because all admissible ordered pairs have been
exhausted and every other pile is empty.

Thus we have exhibited a sequence of \(14\,712\,977\) admissible operations,
showing that the upper bound (1) is attainable.

