        The piles are  

\[
S_{1},S_{2},\dots ,S_{7671},\qquad |S_{k}|=k\;(k=1,\dots ,7671).
\]

When an ordered pair \((i,j)\;(i<j)\) is chosen we remove  

\[
g=\gcd (|S_i|,|S_j|)
\]

coins from each of the two piles.  
Hence every time a pile participates in an operation its number of coins
decreases by **at least** one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound

Pile \(k\) can be used at most \(k\) times, because after \(k\) uses it would
contain \(0\) coins and could not be chosen any more.
Consequently the total number of “uses’’ of all piles is at most  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{7671} k = \frac{7671\cdot 7672}{2}.
\]

Each operation uses two piles, therefore the number \(M\) of possible
operations satisfies  

\[
2M\le \frac{7671\cdot 7672}{2}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
M\le\Big\lfloor\frac{7671^{2}}{4}\Big\rfloor .
\]

(The inequality is strict when the sum on the left‑hand side is odd; the
floor is required.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A construction attaining the bound  

Define  

\[
E=\{(i,j)\mid 1\le i<j\le 7671,\; i+j>7671\}.
\]

The set \(E\) contains exactly  

\[
\bigl\lfloor\frac{7671^{2}}{4}\bigr\rfloor
\]

unordered pairs; this is a standard count (the pairs are those lying
strictly above the diagonal of an \(7671\times 7671\) matrix).

For a vertex \(i\) the number of incident edges in \(E\) is  

\[
d_i=
\begin{cases}
i      & (i\le \tfrac{7671}{2}),\\[2mm]
i-1    & (i> \tfrac{7671}{2}),
\end{cases}
\]

and clearly \(d_i\le i\) for every \(i\).  
Thus the degree‑constraints coming from the coin numbers are satisfied.

Now we perform the operations in the following order.

*For each \(i\) from \(1\) to \(7671\)*
 *use the edges \((i,j)\in E\) with \(j>i\) after the pile \(S_i\) has been
used exactly \(i-1\) times.*

After the first \(i-1\) uses the pile \(S_i\) contains exactly one coin,
so for every remaining edge \((i,j)\) we have \(\gcd(1,|S_j|)=1\); the
operation removes one coin from each of the two piles.  
Thus every edge of \(E\) can indeed be executed, and we obtain exactly
\(|E|=\bigl\lfloor 7671^{2}/4\bigr\rfloor\) operations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Result

Both the upper bound and the construction give the same value, therefore it
is maximal.

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{7671^{2}}{4}\Bigr\rfloor
= \frac{7671^{2}-1}{4}=14\,711\,060 }
\]

Hence the operation can be performed at most **14 711 060** times.

