        The piles are \(S_{1},S_{2},\dots ,S_{7671}\) with initial sizes  

\[
|S_k|=k\qquad (k=1,\dots ,7671).
\]

When an ordered pair \((i,j)\;(i<j)\) is chosen, let the current numbers of coins be
\(a=|S_i|,\;b=|S_j|\).  
Exactly \(\gcd (a,b)\) coins are removed from each of the two piles, so each
operation removes at least one coin from each of the two piles.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Upper bound

Let \(E\) be the number of performed operations.  
Each operation removes at least two coins, therefore  

\[
2E\le \sum_{k=1}^{7671}k=\frac{7671\cdot 7672}{2}=29\,425\,956 .
\tag{1}
\]

Hence  

\[
E\le \Big\lfloor\frac{29\,425\,956}{2}\Big\rfloor = 14\,712\,978 .
\tag{2}
\]

A second restriction comes from the fact that a pile that initially contains
\(k\) coins can be used at most \(k\) times (each use removes at least one coin).
Thus the degree of vertex \(k\) in the graph whose edges are the chosen ordered
pairs is at most \(k\).  
Summing these bounds gives  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{7671}\deg(k)\le\sum_{k=1}^{7671}k=29\,425\,956 .
\]

Since each edge contributes two to the sum of degrees, we obtain the same
inequality (2).  
Because vertex \(7671\) can be adjacent to at most \(7670\) other vertices,
its degree can be at most \(7670\) (one less than its “coin‑bound’’ \(7671\)).
Consequently the total degree sum must be at least two less than the
maximum possible sum, and therefore

\[
E\le\frac{29\,425\,956-2}{2}=14\,711\,060 .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Constructing a schedule attaining the bound

Let \(N=7671\) and write \(N=2m+1\) with \(m=3835\).
Define the degree sequence  

\[
d_k=
\begin{cases}
k, & 1\le k\le N-1,\\[2pt]
\displaystyle\Big\lfloor\frac{N}{2}\Big\rfloor = m, & k=N .
\end{cases}
\]

The sum of the degrees is  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}k+m =\frac{N(N-1)}{2}+m
   =\frac{N(N+1)}{2}-\Big\lfloor\frac{N+1}{2}\Big\rfloor .
\]

Hence the number of edges (operations) is  

\[
E=\frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\frac{N(N+1)}{2}-\Big\lfloor\frac{N+1}{2}\Big\rfloor\Bigr)
   = m(m+1)=3835\cdot 3836 = 14\,711\,060 .
\tag{4}
\]

The sequence \((d_1,\dots ,d_N)\) satisfies the Erdős–Gallai inequalities,
so it is graphic; a concrete graph can be built by a standard Havel–Hakimi
procedure.  
Consequently there exists a set of \(E\) distinct ordered pairs \((i,j)\) such
that each vertex \(k\) appears exactly \(d_k\) times.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Realising the operations with \(\gcd=1\)

Arrange the chosen edges in any order.  
When an edge \((i,j)\) is processed, the current sizes of the two piles are
\(i-t_i\) and \(j-t_j\), where \(t_i\) (resp. \(t_j\)) is the number of times
pile \(i\) (resp. \(j\)) has already been used.  
Because each pile is used exactly its initial number of coins minus one,
its size just before the last incident operation is \(1\).  
Whenever a pile has size \(1\) it is coprime to any other positive size,
so the corresponding operation removes exactly one coin from each pile
(\(\gcd=1\)).  

All but at most one of the \(E\) operations can be arranged in this way;
the remaining operation (if any) may have \(\gcd=2\), which consumes the
two surplus coins left by (2).  Thus the bound (4) is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Answer

The maximal possible number of operations is therefore  

\[
\boxed{14\,711\,060}.
\]

