For each \(N\) the result equals  

\[
\frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\sum_{k=1}^{N}k\Bigr)-\begin{cases}
0 & N\equiv 0,1\pmod 4,\\[2mm]
1 & N\equiv 2,3\pmod 4 .
\end{cases}
\]

Indeed  

* the total number of coins is \(S_N=\frac{N(N+1)}2\);
* each operation removes at least two coins, so we can never exceed \(\lfloor S_N/2\rfloor\);
* a pile can be paired with at most \(N-1\) different piles, therefore the largest pile (size \(N\)) can be used at most \(N-1\) times, which forces a loss of one extra coin when \(N\equiv2,3\pmod4\).

Consequently the maximal possible number of operations for a general \(N\) is  

\[
M(N)=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{S_N- \varepsilon_N}{2}\Bigr\rfloor,
\qquad
\varepsilon_N=
\begin{cases}
0,& N\equiv0,1\pmod4,\\[1mm]
2,& N\equiv2,3\pmod4 .
\end{cases}
\]

For the given problem \(N=7671\).  
Since \(7671\equiv3\pmod4\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{7671}&=\frac{7671\cdot7672}{2}=29\,425\,956,\\[2mm]
M(7671)&=\frac{S_{7671}-2}{2}
        =\frac{29\,425\,956-2}{2}=14\,712\,977 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the greatest possible number of times the operation can be performed is  

\[
\boxed{14\,712\,977}.
\]

