        The piles are \(S_{1},S_{2},\dots ,S_{7671}\) with initial sizes  

\[
|S_k|=k\qquad(k=1,\dots ,7671).
\]

When an ordered pair \((i,j)\;(i<j)\) is chosen, let the current numbers of
coins be \(a\) and \(b\).  The operation removes \(\gcd (a,b)\) coins from each
pile, so at least one coin is removed from each of the two piles.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound

Let \(d_k\) be the number of times pile \(S_k\) is used.
Because each use removes at least one coin from that pile we have  

\[
d_k\le k\qquad (k=1,\dots ,7671).
\]

Each operation uses two piles, therefore  

\[
\text{#operations}= \frac12\sum_{k=1}^{7671} d_k .
\]

Arrange the piles in increasing order of their indices.
The first \(\lfloor N/2\rfloor\) piles can be incident only to the remaining
\(\lceil N/2\rceil\) piles, otherwise an edge would join two piles whose
indices are both \(\le\lfloor N/2\rfloor\) and each of those piles could be
incident to at most \(\lfloor N/2\rfloor\) edges.  Consequently

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{\lfloor N/2\rfloor} d_k\le
\Bigl\lfloor\frac N2\Bigr\rfloor\Bigl\lceil\frac N2\Bigr\rceil .
\]

The same inequality holds for the remaining vertices, and adding the two
inequalities gives  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{N} d_k\le
2\Bigl\lfloor\frac N2\Bigr\rfloor\Bigl\lceil\frac N2\Bigr\rceil .
\]

Hence the number of operations cannot exceed  

\[
\boxed{\Bigl\lfloor\frac N2\Bigr\rfloor\Bigl\lceil\frac N2\Bigr\rceil } .
\]

For \(N=7671\),

\[
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{7671}{2}\Bigr\rfloor=3835,\qquad
\Bigl\lceil\frac{7671}{2}\Bigr\rceil=3836,
\]
so the upper bound is  

\[
3835\times 3836 = 14\,711\,060 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A construction attaining the bound

Write \(N=2m+1\) (here \(m=3835\)).  
Split the piles into  

\[
A=\{m+2,m+3,\dots ,2m+1\}\;( |A|=m),\qquad
B=\{1,2,\dots ,m+1\}\;( |B|=m+1).
\]

Every vertex of \(A\) has capacity at least \(m+1\) (its initial size is
\(\ge m+2\)), and every vertex of \(B\) has capacity at least \(m\)
(the largest one is \(m+1\)).  Hence the complete bipartite graph
\(K_{m,m+1}\) respects the individual capacity limits.

The edges of \(K_{m,m+1}\) can be decomposed into the \(m+1\) perfect
matchings  

\[
M_t=\bigl\{(a,\,b)\mid a\in A,\;b\equiv a+t\pmod{m+1}\bigr\},
\qquad t=0,1,\dots ,m .
\]

In round \(t\) we perform all operations belonging to \(M_t\).
During round \(t\) each pile in \(A\) has already been used \(t\) times,
so its current size is \(a-t\); a pile in \(B\) has been used either \(t\)
or \(t-1\) times, hence its current size is either \(b-t\) or \(b-t+1\).
In either case the two numbers differ by exactly one, i.e. they are
consecutive integers, and therefore coprime.  Consequently every
operation removes exactly one coin from each of the two piles, so the
capacity constraints are never violated.

All edges of \(K_{m,m+1}\) are used exactly once; the number of
operations performed is \(|E(K_{m,m+1})| = m(m+1)\).

Thus the bound is attainable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Result for the given \(N\)

\[
\boxed{14\,711\,060}
\]

Hence the maximum possible number of operations is **14 711 060**.

