        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let }S=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i,\qquad 
F(a_1,\dots ,a_n)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Bigl|\frac{S-a_i}{n}-a_i\Bigr|
      =\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl|S-(n+1)a_i\bigr|.
\end{aligned}
\]

The feasible set \([-1,1]^n\) is a convex polytope and each term 
\(|S-(n+1)a_i|\) is a convex function of the vector \((a_1,\dots ,a_n)\).
Hence \(F\) is convex, and a convex function attains its maximum over a
convex compact set at an extreme point of the set.  
The extreme points of \([-1,1]^n\) are the vertices where each \(a_i\) is
\(-1\) or \(1\).  
Thus we may restrict to \(a_i\in\{-1,1\}\).

Let \(p\) be the number of \(a_i=1\) and \(q=n-p\) the number of
\(a_i=-1\). Then  

\[
S=p-q=2p-n .
\]

For a component equal to \(1\),

\[
|S-(n+1)\cdot1|=|2p-n-(n+1)|
               =|2(p-n)-1|=2q+1 .
\]

For a component equal to \(-1\),

\[
|S-(n+1)(-1)|=|2p-n+(n+1)|
               =|2p+1|=2p+1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}|S-(n+1)a_i|
   =p(2q+1)+q(2p+1)=4pq+n .
\]

Therefore  

\[
f(n)=\frac{1}{n}\bigl(4pq+n\bigr)=\frac{4pq}{n}+1 .
\]

Since \(p+q=n\), the product \(pq\) is maximised when \(p\) and \(q\) are
as close as possible:
\[
\max pq=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\Bigr\rfloor .
\]

Consequently  

\[
f(n)=1+\frac{4\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor}{n}
   =\begin{cases}
      n+1, & n\ \text{even},\\[4pt]
      n+1-\dfrac1n, & n\ \text{odd}.
     \end{cases}
\]

Now compute  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} k f(k)
   =\sum_{k=1}^{225} k(k+1)-\sum_{\substack{k\le225\\k\text{ odd}}}1 .
\]

The first sum is  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} (k^{2}+k)
   =\frac{225\cdot226\cdot(2\cdot225+1)}{6}
    +\frac{225\cdot226}{2}
   =3\,847\,650 .
\]

Among the numbers \(1,\dots ,225\) there are
\(\displaystyle\frac{225+1}{2}=113\) odd integers, so we subtract \(113\).

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} k f(k)=3\,847\,650-113=3\,847\,537 .
\]

\[
