        **Solution Overview**

For a fixed \(n\) and numbers \(a_1,\dots ,a_n\in[-1,1]\) let  

\[
S=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i ,\qquad 
T=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Bigl|\frac{S-a_i}{n}-a_i\Bigr|
      =\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl|S-(n+1)a_i\bigr|.
\]

Thus maximizing \(T\) is equivalent to maximizing  

\[
G:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl|S-(n+1)a_i\bigr|
\tag{1}
\]

subject to \(a_i\in[-1,1]\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The optimum occurs at the endpoints \(\pm1\)

The expression in (1) is a sum of absolute‑value linear functions of each
\(a_i\).  
For a fixed vector \((a_1,\dots ,a_{i-1},a_{i+1},\dots ,a_n)\) the term
containing \(a_i\) is  

\[
|S-(n+1)a_i|
   =\bigl|(S-a_i)-(n+1-1)a_i\bigr|
   =\bigl|C-(n+1)a_i\bigr|
\]

with a constant \(C\).  As a function of \(a_i\) this is a V‑shaped line,
hence its maximum on the interval \([-1,1]\) is attained at an endpoint.
Applying this argument to every coordinate shows that an optimal
configuration uses only the values \(-1\) and \(+1\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reduce to a counting problem

Let  

* \(m\) = number of indices with \(a_i=+1\);
* \(n-m\) = number with \(a_i=-1\).

Then  

\[
S = m\cdot 1 + (n-m)(-1)=2m-n .
\]

For a \(+1\) entry  

\[
|S-(n+1)\cdot 1|
   =|2m-n-(n+1)|
   =|2(m-n)-1|
   =2(n-m)+1 .
\]

For a \(-1\) entry  

\[
|S-(n+1)(-1)|
   =|2m-n+(n+1)|
   =|2m+1|
   =2m+1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
G(m) &=
   m\bigl(2(n-m)+1\bigr)+(n-m)(2m+1)\\[2mm]
      &=4m(n-m)+n .
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximising \(G(m)\)

The term \(4m(n-m)\) is a concave quadratic in \(m\); it is maximal when
\(m\) is as close as possible to \(n/2\).  
Therefore  

\[
\max_{0\le m\le n} G(m)=
   n+4\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rfloor
        \Bigl\lceil\frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rceil .
\]

Using \(\bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\bigr\rfloor\bigl\lceil\frac{n}{2}\bigr\rceil
   =\bigl\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\bigr\rfloor\),

\[
\max G = n+4\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\Bigr\rfloor .
\]

Recall \(f(n)=\dfrac{1}{n}\max G\).  Consequently

\[
\boxed{%
f(n)=1+\frac{4}{n}\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\Bigr\rfloor } .
\]

Writing it separately for even and odd \(n\):

\[
f(n)=
\begin{cases}
n+1, & n\text{ even},\\[2mm]
n+1-\dfrac1n, & n\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required sum

We need  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} k\,f(k)
   =\sum_{k=1}^{225}k\bigl(k+1\bigr)
    -\sum_{\substack{k\le225\\k\text{ odd}}}1 .
\]

The first sum is \(\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{225}(k^{2}+k)
   =\Bigl(\frac{225\cdot226\cdot451}{6}\Bigr)
    +\Bigl(\frac{225\cdot226}{2}\Bigr)
   =3\,822\,225+25\,425
   =3\,847\,650.\)

The number of odd integers from \(1\) to \(225\) is \(\frac{225+1}{2}=113\).

Therefore  

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{225} k\,f(k)=3\,847\,650-113=3\,847\,537 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Final answer

\[
\boxed{3847537}
\]

