        \[
\begin{aligned}
&\angle P=90^\circ,\;\angle Q=105^\circ,\;\angle R=90^\circ,\\
&PQ=2,\; QR=RS=ST=\sqrt2 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Traverse the pentagon \(P\to Q\to R\to S\to T\to P\) counter‑clockwise.
For a convex polygon the **external turn** at a vertex equals \(180^\circ-\) (interior angle).

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{turn at }P &= 90^\circ,\\
\text{turn at }Q &= 75^\circ,\\
\text{turn at }R &= 90^\circ,\\
\text{turn at }S &= \alpha,\\
\text{turn at }T &= \beta .
\end{aligned}
\]

Since \(\angle S+\angle T = 540^\circ-(90^\circ+105^\circ+90^\circ)=255^\circ\),

\[
\alpha+\beta = 360^\circ-(\angle S+\angle T)=105^\circ .
\]

---

### Vector representation
Place \(P\) at the origin and let the side \(PQ\) lie on the positive \(x\)-axis.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec v_1 &= 2\;e^{i0^\circ}=2,\\[2pt]
\vec v_2 &= \sqrt2\,e^{i75^\circ},\\[2pt]
\vec v_3 &= \sqrt2\,e^{i165^\circ},\\[2pt]
\vec v_4 &= \sqrt2\,e^{i(165^\circ+\alpha)},\\[2pt]
\vec v_5 &= PT\;e^{i(165^\circ+\alpha+\beta)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Because \(\alpha+\beta=105^\circ\),

\[
165^\circ+\alpha+\beta = 165^\circ+105^\circ = 270^\circ ,
\]

so \(\vec v_5\) points straight down: \(\vec v_5 = -i\,PT\).

The polygon closes, so  

\[
\vec v_1+\vec v_2+\vec v_3+\vec v_4+\vec v_5 = 0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\vec v_1+\vec v_2+\vec v_3+\vec v_4 = i\,PT .
\]

Hence the sum of the first four vectors must be purely imaginary.

---

### Computing the sum of the first three vectors
Using \(\cos75^\circ=\frac{\sqrt2(\sqrt3-1)}{4},\;
\sin75^\circ=\frac{\sqrt2(\sqrt3+1)}{4}\) and similar values for \(165^\circ\),

\[
\vec v_2 = \frac{\sqrt3-1}{2}+i\frac{\sqrt3+1}{2},\qquad
\vec v_3 = -\frac{\sqrt3+1}{2}+i\frac{\sqrt3-1}{2}.
\]

Thus  

\[
\vec v_1+\vec v_2+\vec v_3 = 1+i\sqrt3 .
\]

---

### Determining \(\alpha\)

Let \(\vec v_4 = \sqrt2\bigl(\cos(165^\circ+\alpha)+i\sin(165^\circ+\alpha)\bigr)\).
The real part of the total sum must vanish:

\[
\operatorname{Re}\bigl(1+i\sqrt3+\vec v_4\bigr)=0
\Longrightarrow
1+\sqrt2\cos(165^\circ+\alpha)=0 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\cos(165^\circ+\alpha)=-\frac{1}{\sqrt2}=-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}.
\]

The angle whose cosine equals \(-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\) in the range \([0^\circ,360^\circ)\) are \(135^\circ\) and \(225^\circ\).
Only \(225^\circ\) works because \(165^\circ+\alpha\) must exceed \(165^\circ\):

\[
165^\circ+\alpha = 225^\circ \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \alpha = 60^\circ .
\]

Consequently \(\beta =105^\circ-\alpha =45^\circ\).

Now  

\[
\vec v_4 = \sqrt2\bigl(\cos225^\circ+i\sin225^\circ\bigr)
          = \sqrt2\left(-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}-i\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)
          = -1-i .
\]

Adding this to the previous sum:

\[
\vec v_1+\vec v_2+\vec v_3+\vec v_4
= (1+i\sqrt3)+(-1-i)= i(\sqrt3-1).
\]

Thus  

\[
i\,PT = i(\sqrt3-1)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad PT = \sqrt3-1 .
\]

---

The length \(PT\) is of the form \(\sqrt{a}-b\) with \(a=3,\;b=1\).

\[
\boxed{a+b = 3+1 = 4}
\]

