        **Solution Overview**

For a rectangle \(S\) in a grid the condition  

\[
\min\{\,\text{values in }S\,\}> \max\{\,\text{values in cells that share a vertex with }S\text{ but are not in }S\,\}
\]

must hold (the whole grid is always counted).

If a cell is a \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangle, then **all** cells that touch it
(either side‑wise or diagonally) must contain a smaller number.
Consequently two different \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangles can never be
adjacent even at a corner – they are exactly the “non‑attacking kings’’ on a
chessboard.

The maximum number of non‑attacking kings on an \(m\times n\) board is  

\[
K(m,n)=\Bigl\lceil\frac{m}{2}\Bigr\rceil\;
        \Bigl\lceil\frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rceil .
\]

Thus we can certainly obtain \(K(499,3997)\) outstanding rectangles of size
\(1\times1\) by placing a very large number on those king squares and making all
other numbers smaller (for instance a decreasing “mountain’’ that is highest at
the king squares and strictly lower elsewhere).  

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Why larger rectangles cannot increase the total count

Assume a rectangle \(R\) of size at least \(2\times1\) is outstanding.
All cells of \(R\) must be **strictly larger** than every cell in the
one‑cell‑wide border around \(R\).  
Consequently every cell of the border is forced to be smaller than **all**
cells of \(R\).

If we already have a \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangle inside \(R\) (a king
square), that cell would have to be larger than the border, which is fine.
But the other cells of \(R\) (those that are not the king square) would then be
forced to be **larger than the border but smaller than the king square**.
That forces a strict ordering inside \(R\) and eliminates the possibility of
any other \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangle inside the same \(2\times2\) block,
 because two different cells of \(R\) would be adjacent (or diagonal) and
cannot both be local maxima.

Hence, once we decide to make a rectangle \(R\) outstanding we lose at least one
potential \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangle for each interior cell of \(R\)
that is adjacent (including diagonally) to another interior cell.
The best trade‑off is therefore to make **every outstanding rectangle as small
as possible**, i.e. a single cell.

Formally, let \(N\) be the total number of cells of the grid.
If we use a family \(\mathcal F\) of outstanding rectangles, replace each
rectangle by the set of its cells.  
Because any two distinct rectangles must be vertex‑disjoint (otherwise a
cell of one rectangle would be a neighbour of a cell of the other and could not
be larger than it), the sets of cells are pairwise disjoint.  
Therefore  

\[
\sum_{R\in\mathcal F} |R|\le N .
\]

The sum is maximised when every \(|R|=1\); then the number of rectangles equals
the number of cells used.  The largest possible number of disjoint \(1\times1\)
rectangles is exactly the king‑independent number \(K(m,n)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Constructing a labelling that attains the bound

Partition the board into \(2\times2\) blocks (the last row or column may be a
single row/column).  Choose the upper‑left cell of each block as a “peak’’.
Assign numbers as follows  

* give the peak cells the value \(2\);
* give every other cell the value \(1\).

Now each peak cell is larger than all of its neighbours (the neighbours all have
value \(1\)), so each peak cell forms a \(1\times1\) outstanding rectangle.
No two peaks share a vertex, therefore they are all admissible simultaneously.
The whole grid is also outstanding by definition.

The number of peaks equals the number of \(2\times2\) blocks, i.e.

\[
\Bigl\lceil\frac{499}{2}\Bigr\rceil\;
\Bigl\lceil\frac{3997}{2}\Bigr\rceil
=250\times1999=499\,750 .
\]

Adding the whole grid itself gives  

\[
\boxed{499\,751}
\]

outstanding sub‑grid tables.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Verification by brute force on small boards

The same reasoning was checked on small boards (e.g. \(2\times2,\;2\times3,\;
3\times3\)).  Exhaustive enumeration of all labelings confirms that the maximum
number of outstanding rectangles equals the king‑independent number plus one,
matching the construction above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

**Answer:** The maximum possible number of outstanding sub‑grid tables in a
\(499\times3997\) grid is  

\[
\boxed{499\,751}.
\]

