        The number of outstanding sub‑grid tables is maximized when the single global maximum of the grid is placed at a cell that belongs to the largest possible number of rectangles.  

For a rectangular grid of size \(R\times C\) and a cell at row \(r\) and column \(c\),

\[
\#\text{rectangles containing the cell}=r\,(R-r+1)\,c\,(C-c+1).
\]

This product is maximized when the cell is as central as possible.  
Since \(R=499\) (odd) and \(C=3997\) (odd),

\[
r=c=\frac{R+1}{2}=250,\qquad
c=\frac{C+1}{2}=1999 .
\]

Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
\#_{\max}
&=250\,(499-250+1)\; \times\; 1999\,(3997-1999+1)\\[2mm]
&=250\cdot250\;\times\;1999\cdot1999\\[2mm]
&=62\,500\;\times\;3\,996\,001\\[2mm]
&=249\,750\,062\,500 .
\end{aligned}
\]

A construction that attains this bound is to assign the global maximum to this central cell and let the numbers strictly decrease with Manhattan distance from it; then every rectangle containing the central cell satisfies the “outstanding’’ condition, while any rectangle not containing the global maximum cannot be outstanding.

\[
\boxed{249\,750\,062\,500}
\]

