a_{k}\,k! = a_{k-1}\,(k-1)!+k^{\,n-1}\,k!
\]

and, iterating,

\[
a_{k}\,k! = a+\sum_{i=1}^{k} i^{\,n-1}\,i! .
\tag{1}
\]

Hence  

\[
a_{k}\in\mathbb Z\Longleftrightarrow 
a+\sum_{i=1}^{k} i^{\,n-1}\,i!\equiv 0\pmod{k!}\qquad\forall k\ge 1 .
\tag{2}
\]

Denote  

\[
T_{k}= \sum_{i=1}^{k} i^{\,n-1}\,i!.
\]

From (2) we need an integer \(a\) satisfying  

\[
a\equiv -T_{k}\pmod{k!}\qquad (k=1,2,\dots ).
\tag{3}
\]

The congruences are compatible, because  

\[
T_{k+1}=T_{k}+(k+1)^{\,n-1}(k+1)!
      =T_{k}+(k+1)^{\,n}\,k!
\]

and the extra term \((k+1)^{\,n}k!\) is a multiple of \(k!\).  
Thus  

\[
-T_{k+1}\equiv -T_{k}\pmod{k!},
\]

so the residue required modulo \(k!\) already agrees with the residue
required modulo \((k-1)!\). Consequently the system (3) has a solution;
by the Chinese‑remainder theorem there exists an integer \(a\) that
satisfies all congruences simultaneously.

Therefore **for every positive integer \(n\) one can find an integer \(a\) such that the whole sequence \(\{a_k\}\) consists of integers**.

Since the statement holds for all \(n\), the remainder of \(n\) upon division by \(3\) can be any of the three possible values.

\[
\boxed{\,0,\;1,\;2\,}
\]

