        [Turn 58 REASONING] (2419 chars)
        \[
\begin{cases}
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3,\\[2mm]
xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}=3 .
\end{cases}
\]

Let  

\[
S_{1}=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2},\qquad   
S_{2}=xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}.
\]

Both equations describe algebraic surfaces in \(\mathbb R^{3}\); their
intersection is the set of required triples.

---

### 1.  Elimination of one variable  

From the first equation we have  

\[
z^{2}=3-x^{2}-y^{2},\qquad 3-x^{2}-y^{2}\ge 0 .
\]

Write \(z=\varepsilon\sqrt{3-x^{2}-y^{2}}\) with \(\varepsilon=\pm1\).
Substituting in the second equation gives  

\[
xy^{3}+y\varepsilon (3-x^{2}-y^{2})^{\frac32}
   +x\varepsilon (3-x^{2}-y^{2})^{\frac32}=3 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{\,xy^{3}+ \varepsilon (x+y)(3-x^{2}-y^{2})^{\frac32}=3\,}.
\tag{1}
\]

Squaring (1) eliminates the square‑root and yields a single polynomial
relation between \(x\) and \(y\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
&(3-x^{2}-y^{2})\,(x^{3}+y^{3})^{2}-(3-xy^{3})^{2}=0\\[2mm]
&\Longleftrightarrow\;
x^{8}+x^{6}y^{2}-3x^{6}-4x^{5}y^{3}+12x^{5}y
   +3x^{4}y^{4}-9x^{4}y^{2}\\
&\qquad\; -2x^{3}y^{5}+12x^{3}y^{3}-18x^{3}y
   +4x^{2}y^{6}-18x^{2}y^{4}+27x^{2}y^{2}
   -6xy^{3}+y^{8}-9y^{6}+27y^{4}-27y^{2}+9=0 .
\tag{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

Equation (2) is a non‑degenerate algebraic curve in the \((x,y)\)-plane.
Because it is a single polynomial equation in two variables, its set of
real points is one‑dimensional (a curve).  For every point \((x,y)\) on
this curve with \(x^{2}+y^{2}\le 3\) we obtain a real \(z\) from  

\[
z=\pm\sqrt{3-x^{2}-y^{2}},
\]

and (1) guarantees that the original second equation is also satisfied.
Consequently each point of the curve gives a real triple \((x,y,z)\).

---

### 2.  Existence of infinitely many points  

The curve (2) indeed possesses real points.  For example, taking  

\[
x\approx 0.2846149572,\qquad y\approx 0.9241389611
\]

satisfies (2).  Then  

\[
z=\sqrt{3-x^{2}-y^{2}}\approx 1.436997393
\]

and one checks

\[
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3,\qquad
xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}=3 .
\]

Because (2) is a genuine algebraic curve, it contains infinitely many
real points; varying the point on the curve continuously varies the
corresponding \(z\).  Hence the original system has infinitely many
real solutions.

(The only solutions with \(x=y=z\) are \((1,1,1)\) and \((-1,-1,-1)\); they
are just two points on the same curve.)

---

\[
\boxed{\text{There are infinitely many ordered triples }(x,y,z)\in\mathbb R^{3}
\text{ satisfying the two equations.}}
\]

