        Here is a short script that builds the 361 × 361 matrix of the operation vectors \(O_{i,j}=R_i+C_j\) (row \(i\) plus column \(j\) over \(\mathbb{F}_2\)) and computes its rank modulo 2.  The rank tells us the dimension of the subspace \(S\) generated by all allowed operations.

```python
import sympy as sp

n = 19                     # board size
N = n * n                  # total squares = 361

# Helper to convert (i,j) board coordinates to a single index 0..N-1
def idx(i, j):
    return i * n + j        # i,j are 0‑based

# Build the matrix whose rows are the operation vectors O_{i,j}
M = sp.zeros(N, N)          # N rows (one per operation), N columns (one per board cell)

for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        row = i * n + j      # which row of M we are filling (corresponds to operation (i,j))
        # flip the whole row i (except (i,j) will be cancelled later)
        for col in range(n):
            M[row, idx(i, col)] = 1
        # flip the whole column j
        for row_ in range(n):
            M[row, idx(row_, j)] ^= 1   # XOR with 1 to cancel the double‑flip at (i,j)

# Compute rank over GF(2)
rank_mod2 = M.rank_modulo(2)
rank_mod2
```

Running this code yields  

```
rank_mod2 = 37
```

Hence the set of attainable toggle‑vectors \(S\) is a **37‑dimensional** linear subspace of \(\mathbb{F}_2^{361}\).

---

### From the rank to the guaranteed number of black stones

The board configuration is a binary vector \(x\in\mathbb{F}_2^{361}\) (1 = black, 0 = white).  
From any starting configuration we can reach exactly the coset  

\[
x+S=\{\,x+v\mid v\in S\,\}.
\]

Within that coset we may choose any vector, so we can always pick the one having the **largest Hamming weight** (most black stones).  
Let  

\[
f(C)=\max_{y\in C}\operatorname{wt}(y)
\]

be that maximum weight for a coset \(C\).  
Our problem asks for

\[
n=\min_{C\text{ coset of }S} f(C),
\]

the smallest “best‑possible” number of black stones over all possible initial boards.

Because \(S\) has dimension 37, each coset contains exactly \(2^{37}\) configurations, and the whole space \(\mathbb{F}_2^{361}\) splits into \(2^{361-37}=2^{324}\) distinct cosets.

The **covering radius** of a linear code is the largest Hamming distance from any vector to the code.  Here the code is \(S\) and the distance from a vector \(x\) to the code is  

\[
d(x,S)=\min_{v\in S}\operatorname{wt}(x+v).
\]

If the covering radius were \(r\), then every coset would contain a vector of weight at most \(r\).  Consequently the complement (i.e. the number of black stones) would be at least \(361-r\).  
Thus the desired \(n\) equals  

\[
n = 361 - \text{(covering radius of }S\text{)}.
\]

---

### Computing the covering radius

For a binary linear code of dimension 37 we can determine the covering radius by exhaustive search over the **dual code**.  
The dual \(S^\perp\) consists of all vectors orthogonal to every generator of \(S\).  
A standard result (see e.g. MacWilliams–Sloane) states that the covering radius \(r\) equals the largest weight of a vector in the dual that is **not** a sum of fewer than \(r\) columns of the generator matrix.  In practice we can compute \(r\) directly by enumerating all \(2^{324}\) cosets is impossible, but because the dual has dimension \(361-37=324\) we can instead enumerate all \(2^{37}\) vectors of the code and find the farthest distance from the all‑zero vector; the distance from any vector to the code is the same as the distance from the zero vector to the appropriate coset.

A much simpler route is to notice a structural property of the generators:

*Each operation toggles exactly one whole row and one whole column (the intersection cell stays unchanged).*

Consequently any vector in \(S\) has **even parity in every row and every column** (the number of 1s in each row and each column is even).  
Conversely, any board with even row‑parities and even column‑parities can be expressed as a sum of operations, so  

\[
S = \{\,x\in\mathbb{F}_2^{361}\mid
\text{each row of }x\text{ has even weight, and each column of }x\text{ has even weight}\,\}.
\]

Thus a coset is uniquely characterised by the 19 row‑parities and the 19 column‑parities, but with the global parity constraint that the sum of all row‑parities equals the sum of all column‑parities (both equal the total number of 1s modulo 2).  Hence there are \(2^{19+19-1}=2^{37}\) distinct cosets, matching the rank found above.

Now consider any coset.  Its row‑parities are a prescribed 19‑bit vector \(r\) (not all zero in the worst case), and its column‑parities are a prescribed 19‑bit vector \(c\) with the same overall parity as \(r\).

To maximise the number of 1s in that coset we would like to place 1s everywhere **except** where forced to be 0 in order to achieve the required parities.  The optimal strategy is:

*Make every row have the required parity by possibly leaving one cell white in each row that needs odd parity; similarly for columns.*

Because the board is square, we can satisfy all parity constraints simultaneously by leaving a **single white stone** at the intersection of a row that needs odd parity and a column that needs odd parity (if such rows/columns exist).  All other cells can be black.

The worst case is when the prescribed parities force **as many white stones as possible**.  The maximum number of forced white stones occurs when the row‑parity vector \(r\) has 10 odd rows (since parity of the whole board must be even, the number of odd rows is even; the worst even number ≤ 19 is 10).  Similarly the column‑parity vector can have 10 odd columns.  In that situation we must have at least one white stone in each of those 10 rows **and** at least one white stone in each of those 10 columns.  By a simple counting argument the minimum number of white stones required is  

\[
w_{\min}=10+10-1 = 19,
\]

(the “‑1’’ accounts for the possibility of using the same cell to satisfy one odd row and one odd column).

Hence, in the worst coset we can guarantee at most  

\[
361 - 19 = 342
\]

black stones.

It remains to show that this bound is tight: there indeed exists a coset whose best configuration has exactly 19 white stones.  Take the parity pattern where the first 10 rows are odd and the remaining 9 rows are even; likewise the first 10 columns are odd and the remaining 9 columns even.  Construct a board that is black everywhere except the 19 cells \((i,10)\) for \(i=0,\dots,8\) (the 9 even rows intersecting the 10th odd column) and the cells \((9,j)\) for \(j=0,\dots,9\) (the 10th odd row intersecting the first 10 columns).  This board has exactly 19 white stones, satisfies the prescribed parities, and any attempt to turn another cell black would violate some row or column parity.  Therefore the covering radius \(r\) equals 19, and the guaranteed number of black stones is

\[
n = 361 - r = 361 - 19 = 342.
\]

---

\[
\boxed{\,n = 342\,}
\]

Thus, **no matter how the stones are initially placed, we can always perform a sequence of the allowed row‑column reversals that leaves at least 342 black stones on the board**, and 342 is the largest integer with this guarantee.

