        \[
\text{Board size}=19\times 19,\qquad N=19^{2}=361 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Linear description of an operation  

Write a board as a binary vector \(x\in\mathbb F_{2}^{361}\)  
(\(1\)=black, \(0\)=white).  

Choosing a row \(i\) and a column \(j\) flips the colour of the
\(19+19-1=37\) squares belonging to the union of that row and column.
The corresponding vector is  

\[
v_{i,j}=R_i+C_j+E_{i,j},
\]

where  

* \(R_i\) – the matrix whose \(i\)-th row is all‑1’s,
* \(C_j\) – the matrix whose \(j\)-th column is all‑1’s,
* \(E_{i,j}\) – the matrix with a single 1 at \((i,j)\).

All operations are performed modulo 2, therefore the set of all
reachable configurations from a given board \(x\) is  

\[
x+S,\qquad 
S=\operatorname{span}\{v_{i,j}\mid 1\le i,j\le 19\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The subspace \(S\)

For a matrix \(M\) let  

\[
\rho_{\text{row}}(M)=\bigl(\sum_j M_{i,j}\bmod 2\bigr)_{i=1}^{19},
\qquad 
\rho_{\text{col}}(M)=\bigl(\sum_i M_{i,j}\bmod 2\bigr)_{j=1}^{19}.
\]

From the definition of \(v_{i,j}\),

\[
\rho_{\text{row}}(v_{i,j})=(1,1,\dots ,1),\qquad
\rho_{\text{col}}(v_{i,j})=(1,1,\dots ,1).
\]

Hence every linear combination of the \(v_{i,j}\)’s has **the same parity
in every row** and **the same parity in every column**.
Conversely, any \(19\times19\) binary matrix whose rows all have the same
parity \(p\) and whose columns all have the same parity \(p\) (the same
\(p\) for rows and columns) can be written as a combination of the
\(v_{i,j}\)’s.  

Thus  

\[
S=\Bigl\{M\in\mathbb F_{2}^{19\times19}\;
\Big|\;
\text{all rows have parity }p,\;
\text{all columns have parity }p\;(p\in\{0,1\})\Bigr\}.
\]

The conditions give \(2\cdot19-2=36\) independent linear constraints,
so  

\[
\dim S = 361-36 = 325 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Distance to the subspace  

For a board \(x\) denote  

\[
k = \#\{\text{rows of }x\text{ with odd parity}\},\qquad
\ell = \#\{\text{columns of }x\text{ with odd parity}\}.
\]

Adding a vector of \(S\) changes **every** row parity by the same
value \(p\) (and the same for the columns).  
Consequently the two possible parity patterns of vectors in the coset
\(x+S\) are  

\[
(k,\ell)\qquad\text{or}\qquad(19-k,\,19-\ell).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimal Hamming distance from a coset to the all‑black board  

A matrix whose rows have odd‑parity set \(k\) and columns odd‑parity set
\(\ell\) must contain at least  

\[
\max(k,\ell)
\]

ones; this is attained by connecting each odd‑row with an odd‑column
(and, if the numbers differ, using a common even column for the excess
odd rows).  
Hence the smallest Hamming distance from the coset \(x+S\) to the
all‑black board \(J\) is  

\[
d(x)=\min\bigl(\max(k,\ell),\; \max(19-k,\,19-\ell)\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Covering radius of \(S\)

The covering radius \(R\) of the code \(S\) is  

\[
R=\max_{x} d(x)
   =\max_{\substack{0\le k,\ell\le 19\\ k\equiv\ell\pmod 2}}
     \min\bigl(\max(k,\ell),\;19-\min(k,\ell)\bigr).
\]

Because \(19\) is odd, the parity condition forces \(k\) and \(\ell\) to
have the same parity.  
The maximum of the expression is attained for  

\[
(k,\ell)=(0,18)\ \text{or}\ (18,0),
\]

giving  

\[
R = \min\bigl(\max(0,18),\;19-\min(0,18)\bigr)=18 .
\]

Thus **every** board can be brought within Hamming distance \(18\) of the
all‑black board, and there exist boards (e.g. all rows even and exactly
\(18\) columns odd) that are at distance exactly \(18\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Guaranteed number of black stones  

If a board is at distance \(d\) from the all‑black board, after the
optimal sequence of operations we can obtain a board with
\(361-d\) black stones.  
Since the worst possible distance is \(R=18\),

\[
\boxed{n = 361 - 18 = 343 } .
\]

So, **no matter how the 361 stones are initially coloured, we can always
perform a sequence of the allowed operations that leaves at least
\(343\) black stones on the board, and this bound is best possible.**

