        [Turn 9 REASONING] (1900 chars)
        The Manhattan distance between the two pieces is  

\[
D=|x_W-x_B|+|y_W-y_B|.
\]

Because each piece must alternate between a horizontal and a vertical move, after any two successive moves (one by Alice and one by Bob) the parity of the Manhattan distance does not change.  
Alice moves first, so after her move the parity of \(D\) is opposite to the parity before her move, and after Bob’s move it returns to the original parity.  

Consequently, Bob can capture the white piece **iff the initial Manhattan distance is even**; otherwise the parity will always be odd after Bob’s moves and the pieces can never meet.

Hence the problem reduces to finding the probability that two distinct cells of a \(2025\times2025\) board have the same parity of the sum of their coordinates.

---

### Counting cells of each parity  

Let the board coordinates be \((i,j)\) with \(1\le i,j\le 2025\).  
Define the colour (parity) of a cell by the parity of \(i+j\).

Because the board size is odd, the numbers of even‑coloured and odd‑coloured cells are  

\[
E=\frac{2025^{2}+1}{2},\qquad O=\frac{2025^{2}-1}{2}.
\]

---

### Ordered pairs with even Manhattan distance  

The Manhattan distance is even exactly when the two cells have the same colour.  
The number of ordered distinct pairs with the same colour is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_{\text{even}}&=E(E-1)+O(O-1)\\
&=\frac{(2025^{2}+1)(2025^{2}-1)}{4}
   +\frac{(2025^{2}-1)(2025^{2}-3)}{4}\\[2mm]
&=\frac{(2025^{2}-1)^{2}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The total number of ordered distinct pairs of cells is  

\[
N_{\text{total}}=2025^{2}\bigl(2025^{2}-1\bigr).
\]

Thus the required probability is  

\[
p=\frac{N_{\text{even}}}{N_{\text{total}}}
   =\frac{2025^{2}-1}{2\cdot2025^{2}}
   =\frac{4100624}{8201250}
   =\frac{2050312}{4100625},
\]

where the fraction is already in lowest terms.

Hence \(p=m/n\) with \(\gcd(m,n)=1\) and  

\[
\boxed{m=2050312}.
\]

