        The board is a \(2025\times 2025\) lattice.  
Let the white piece start at \((x_{w},y_{w})\) and the black piece at \((x_{b},y_{b})\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  When can Bob capture?

Both pieces move one cell per turn and must alternate the direction of their moves  
(horizontal – vertical – horizontal – …).  

After \(k\) moves of a piece

* it has made \(\lceil k/2\rceil\) moves in the direction of its first move,
* \(\lfloor k/2\rfloor\) moves in the orthogonal direction.

Hence after an **even** number \(k=2t\) of moves the piece has performed exactly
\(t\) horizontal and \(t\) vertical steps.  
Its position after \(2t\) moves is therefore

\[
(x_{0}\pm a,\;y_{0}\pm b),\qquad a+b=t .
\]

Consequently, after any equal number of moves the two pieces can be in the same
cell **iff** the Manhattan distance between their starting cells is even.
Indeed, if the distance \(D=|x_{w}-x_{b}|+|y_{w}-y_{b}|\) is even, both
players can choose the first direction so that after each pair of moves the
parities of the horizontal and vertical displacements match, and Bob can
force a capture.  
If \(D\) is odd, the parity can never become the same, and Alice can always
keep the pieces apart.  

Thus

\[
\boxed{\text{Bob has a winning strategy } \Longleftrightarrow
      \text{ the Manhattan distance of the initial cells is even}.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Counting the favourable initial positions

A Manhattan distance is even exactly when the two cells have the same colour
on a chessboard colouring (cells with \((x+y)\) even or odd).  

On an odd‑sized board \(N\times N\) (\(N=2025\))

\[
\begin{aligned}
\#\text{even‑coloured cells}&=E=\frac{N^{2}+1}{2},\\
\#\text{odd‑coloured cells }&=O=\frac{N^{2}-1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Ordered pairs of distinct cells of the same colour are

\[
E(E-1)+O(O-1)=\frac{(N^{2}-1)^{2}}{2}.
\]

The total number of ordered pairs of distinct cells is

\[
N^{4}-N^{2}=N^{2}(N^{2}-1).
\]

Therefore the required probability is

\[
p=\frac{\dfrac{(N^{2}-1)^{2}}{2}}{N^{2}(N^{2}-1)}
   =\frac{N^{2}-1}{2N^{2}}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Applying \(N=2025\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
N^{2}&=2025^{2}=4\,100\,625,\\[2mm]
p&=\frac{4\,100\,625-1}{2\cdot4\,100\,625}
   =\frac{4\,100\,624}{8\,201\,250}
   =\frac{2\,050\,312}{4\,100\,625}\quad(\text{reduced}).
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence \(p=\dfrac{m}{n}\) with \(m=2\,050\,312\) and \(n=4\,100\,625\).

\[
\boxed{m=2050312}
\]

