        The only thing that matters is the parity of the sum of the coordinates of the two pieces.

* Let a cell \((x,y)\) have **even** parity if \(x+y\) is even and **odd** otherwise.  
* After each move a piece changes the parity of its own cell (a step horizontally or vertically flips the parity).  
* Alice moves first, so after her move the two pieces have opposite parity.  
* Bob moves next; his move also flips his parity. Hence just before Bob’s move the pieces must have **different** parity, and after his move (if he lands on the white piece) they have the same parity.

Consequently, for Bob ever to be able to capture the white piece, the two pieces must start with the **same parity**.  
If they start with different parity, after every full round (Alice + Bob) the parity relation returns to the original one, so it never becomes the required configuration and Bob can never capture.

When the initial parities are the same, Bob can always force a capture: after Alice’s move the pieces are adjacent (or can be made adjacent in a finite number of rounds) and Bob can move onto the white piece on his turn, respecting the alternating‑direction rule. Hence Bob has a winning strategy **iff** the initial cells have the same parity.

---

### Counting the favourable initial positions

The board is \(2025\times2025\) (odd side length).  
Number of cells  

\[
T = 2025^{2}=4\,100\,625 .
\]

Because the side length is odd, the numbers of even‑parity and odd‑parity cells differ by one:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{even cells} &=\frac{T-1}{2}=2\,050\,312,\\[2mm]
\text{odd cells}  &=\frac{T+1}{2}=2\,050\,313 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Choose the white piece uniformly; the black piece is then chosen uniformly among the remaining \(T-1\) cells.

\[
\begin{aligned}
p &=\frac{\text{even}}{T}\cdot\frac{\text{even}-1}{T-1}
   +\frac{\text{odd}}{T}\cdot\frac{\text{odd}-1}{T-1}\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{2\,050\,312}{4\,100\,625}\cdot\frac{2\,050\,311}{4\,100\,624}
   +\frac{2\,050\,313}{4\,100\,625}\cdot\frac{2\,050\,312}{4\,100\,624}\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{2\,050\,312}{4\,100\,625}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus  

\[
p=\frac{m}{n}= \frac{2\,050\,312}{4\,100\,625},
\qquad\gcd(m,n)=1,
\]

so the required numerator is  

\[
\boxed{\,2\,050\,312\,}.
\]

