        [Turn 23 REASONING] (2007 chars)
        The only invariant of the game is the parity of the sum of the coordinates.

*   A move (horizontal or vertical) changes a piece’s \((x+y)\) by \(\pm1\); therefore after each move the parity of \((x+y)\) flips.
*   After a whole round (Alice’s move and then Bob’s move) each piece has moved exactly once, so both pieces have flipped their parity the same number of times.  
    Consequently the parity difference between the two pieces never changes.

Bob wins only if the two pieces can ever occupy the same cell.  
If the initial cells have opposite parity, the pieces will always have opposite parity, so they can never meet; Alice can avoid capture forever.  

If the initial cells have the same parity, the Manhattan distance between them is even.  
Bob can always move his piece toward the white piece; after each round the distance can be reduced by \(2\).  
Because the distance is even, after finitely many rounds it becomes \(0\); at that moment Bob’s move lands on the white piece and he wins.  

Hence **Bob has a winning strategy iff the two starting cells have the same parity**.

---

### Counting the favourable ordered pairs

On a \(2025\times2025\) board there are \(N=2025\) rows and columns, so \(N^{2}=2025^{2}=4\,100\,625\) cells.

Because \(N\) is odd, the numbers of even‑parity and odd‑parity cells are  

\[
E=\frac{N^{2}+1}{2},\qquad O=\frac{N^{2}-1}{2}.
\]

The ordered pairs of distinct cells with the same parity are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{wins}&=E\,(E-1)+O\,(O-1)\\
&=\frac{(N^{2}+1)(N^{2}-1)}{4}+\frac{(N^{2}-1)(N^{2}-3)}{4}\\[2mm]
&=\frac{N^{2}\bigl(N^{2}-2\bigr)+1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The total number of ordered distinct pairs is  

\[
\text{total}=N^{2}\bigl(N^{2}-1\bigr).
\]

Therefore  

\[
p=\frac{\displaystyle\frac{N^{2}(N^{2}-2)+1}{2}}{N^{2}(N^{2}-1)}
   =\frac{8407558594688}{16815121290000}
   =\frac{2050312}{4100625},
\]

where the fraction has been reduced to lowest terms.

Thus \(p=\dfrac{m}{n}\) with \(\gcd(m,n)=1\) and  

\[
\boxed{m=2050312}.
\]

